// Class 9 English Preloaded NCERT Solutions Data Bundle (All 26 Units)
window.CHAPTERS_DATA = {"1": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch1\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">1</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 1: The Fun They Had</h2>\n      <p>Isaac Asimov — Future Schools vs Old Schools — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Answer Questions (30–40 Words / 2 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How old are Margie and Tommy?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Margie is eleven years old and Tommy is thirteen years old. They are futuristic school children living in the year 2157 who study through computerized telebooks and mechanized domestic teachers.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Margie's age: 11 years</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tommy's age: 13 years</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did Margie write in her diary?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>On the night of 17 May 2157, Margie recorded a remarkable event in her personal diary with the words: <em>'Today Tommy found a real book!'</em> To Margie, a printed paper book was an unprecedented and historical novelty because she had only ever seen moving words on television screens.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Date: 17 May 2157 + exact statement</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Significance: Real paper book was an astonishing novelty in their era</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Had Margie ever seen a book before?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>No, Margie had never seen a real physical book before. She had only heard about printed books from her grandfather, who told her that his grandfather had told him about a long-ago era when all stories were printed exclusively on paper.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Direct negative answer ('No, never')</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reference to her grandfather's account of printed books</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What things about the book did Margie and Tommy find strange?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Margie and Tommy found several aspects of the real book exceptionally strange and curious:</p><ul><li><strong>Yellow and Crinkly Pages:</strong> The physical paper had turned yellow and crumpled over centuries.</li><li><strong>Still Words:</strong> The words stood rigidly still on the page rather than moving across a screen as telebooks did.</li><li><strong>Permanence of Text:</strong> When they turned back to a preceding page, it still contained the identical words they had read previously, unlike digital screens where text constantly refreshed.</li><li><strong>Wastefulness:</strong> Tommy considered it a waste because after reading a printed book, one would simply throw it away, whereas their television screen could hold millions of books.</li></ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Yellow, crinkly pages with static text</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Words remaining unchanged on turning back</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tommy's perspective of wastefulness vs telebook screen</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What do you think a telebook is?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A telebook is an electronic, digital book displayed on a television or computer monitor. The text moves across the screen and can be scrolled. A single television screen or memory unit can store hundreds of thousands or even millions of distinct books without taking up physical space or deteriorating over time.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: digital/electronic book on screen</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Features: moving text, massive storage capacity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where was Margie's school? Did she have any classmates?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Margie's school was situated right inside her own house, in the room right next to her bedroom. She did not have any classmates whatsoever; she studied entirely alone in isolation with her personalized mechanical teacher that was programmed to match her individual learning pace.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Location: Room next to her bedroom in her house</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No classmates: Solo learning with mechanical teacher</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What subjects did Margie and Tommy learn?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Margie and Tommy learned geography, history, and mathematics (arithmetic). Margie was currently struggling with geography tests, while Tommy's mechanical teacher had recently malfunctioned because its history sector had blanked out completely.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Subjects enumerated: Geography, History, Mathematics/Arithmetic</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contextual detail of Margie's geography and Tommy's history sector</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Detailed Analytical Answers (100–120 Words / 5–6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What had once happened to Tommy's mechanical teacher? Why was the County Inspector called to Margie's house?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Tommy's Teacher Malfunction:</strong><br>Once, Tommy's mechanical teacher suffered a major technical failure when its history sector blanked out entirely. The repair personnel had to take the teacher away for almost a full month to overhaul and reprogram the hardware and software systems.</p><p><strong>2. Why County Inspector Was Summoned for Margie:</strong><br>Margie's mother sent for the County Inspector because Margie had been performing consistently poorly in her geography assessments. The mechanical teacher had been giving her test after test in geography, and Margie had been doing worse and worse, sinking into frustration and distress.</p><p><strong>3. The County Inspector's Diagnosis and Repair:</strong><br>The County Inspector—a friendly, round little man with a red face and a box of tools with dials and wires—dismantled the mechanical teacher. He discovered that the geography sector had been geared too rapidly for an average eleven-year-old child. He slowed down its pace to an average ten-year level and reassured Mrs. Jones that Margie's overall progress pattern was quite satisfactory.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tommy's history sector blanking out + 1 month repair</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Margie's declining performance in geography tests</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Inspector's diagnosis: Sector geared too fast, slowed to 10-year level</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e1_9\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e1_9')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q9</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Margie hate school? What were her feelings about the old kind of school described in the book?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Why Margie Hated School:</strong><br>Margie despised school because her education was mechanical, lonely, rigid, and devoid of human warmth. Her classroom was an empty room next to her bedroom where an emotionless mechanical teacher flashed on the screen at fixed hours every day (except weekends). What Margie hated most was the slot where she was required to insert her homework and test papers written in punch code, which the mechanical teacher graded instantaneously in zero seconds, stripping away all joy from learning.</p><p><strong>2. Margie's Fascination with the Old Kind of School:</strong><br>When Margie learned about the schools of centuries ago from Tommy's book, she was enchanted. She discovered that in the old days:</p><ul><li>Teachers were real human beings who explained lessons, asked questions, and empathized with emotions.</li><li>Students from the entire neighborhood walked together to a dedicated special building, laughing and shouting in the schoolyard.</li><li>Children of the same age sat together in common classrooms and learned the exact same syllabus, enabling them to help one another with homework and discuss problems openly.</li></ul><p><strong>Conclusion:</strong><br>Margie sat before her mechanical teacher, daydreaming about how much fun the children must have had in the old days—playing, bonding, and learning together as a vibrant social community.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reasons for hatred: Isolation, mechanical teacher, punch code homework slot</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Old school features: Human teachers, special common building, peer interaction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thematic contrast & emotional yearning: Nostalgia for social learning ('the fun they had')</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 1</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Case Study / Theme Question</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Real-World Connection:</strong> Isaac Asimov wrote 'The Fun They Had' in 1951 predicting digital and remote schooling. How does the story highlight the indispensability of human teachers and social classrooms in holistic child development?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Asimov's story provides a prophetic critique of purely digitized, isolated education. While technology offers customized pacing and vast information storage, it completely fails to fulfill emotional, interpersonal, and psychological needs. Human teachers bring empathy, moral guidance, spontaneous encouragement, and personal understanding that machines cannot replicate. Furthermore, physical classrooms foster collaboration, peer empathy, communication skills, and social camaraderie. Education is not merely data transmission into the brain; it is a collective human experience, proving that technology should assist teachers, never replace them.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Value-Based Question</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Comparison:</strong> Contrast Margie's perception of education with Tommy's attitude towards the ancient book.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Tommy is thirteen, practical, curious, and somewhat condescending towards Margie. He views the physical book through an analytical, utilitarian lens—comparing its finite pages with the superior storage capacity of their million-book telebooks. In contrast, Margie is emotional, introspective, and acutely starved for companionship. While Tommy enjoys discovering historical facts, Margie connects with the human experience behind the facts. She romanticizes the ancient school system because it offered what her privileged yet sterile robotic life lacked: friends, shared laughter, human teachers, and mutual support.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <div></div>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "2": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch2\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">2</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 2: The Sound of Music</h2>\n      <p>Part I: Evelyn Glennie | Part II: Bismillah Khan — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">Part I: Evelyn Glennie Listens to Sound without Hearing It — Exercise Q&A</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How old was Evelyn when she went to the Royal Academy of Music? When was her deafness first noticed and confirmed?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Age at Royal Academy:</strong> Evelyn was seventeen years old when she auditioned for and gained admission to the prestigious Royal Academy of Music in London.</p><p><strong>2. When Deafness was Noticed:</strong> Her hearing impairment was first noticed when she was eight years old. Her mother, Isabel Glennie, observed that Evelyn did not respond when her name was called to play the piano.</p><p><strong>3. When Deafness was Confirmed:</strong> Her deafness was medically confirmed when she was eleven years old. Her deteriorating school grades prompted the headmistress to urge her parents to consult a specialist, who discovered that her hearing was severely damaged as a result of gradual nerve damage.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Age 17 at Royal Academy of Music</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deafness first noticed at age 8 (piano incident)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deafness medically confirmed at age 11 (gradual nerve damage)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who helped Evelyn to continue with music? What did he do and say?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Master percussionist <strong>Ron Forbes</strong> spotted Evelyn's extraordinary potential when others discouraged her. Instead of telling her to listen with her ears, he tuned two large drums to different notes and told her: <em>'Don't listen through your ears, try to sense it some other way.'</em></p><p>Forbes began playing rhythms and encouraged Evelyn to feel the vibrations. Evelyn suddenly discovered that she could sense the higher drum from the waist up and the lower drum from the waist down. Forbes repeated the exercise, and Evelyn unlocked the secret of opening her mind and body to musical vibrations.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification: Percussionist Ron Forbes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Action: Tuned two large drums to different frequencies</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Quote & Guidance: Sense sound through body vibrations instead of ears</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does Evelyn hear music? (Max Word Count / 100–120 Words)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Evelyn Glennie does not hear music through her auditory canals; she feels and senses it throughout her entire body.</p><ul><li><strong>Percussion Vibrations:</strong> When playing drums, she feels higher notes vibrating in the upper half of her torso above the waist and lower resonant notes vibrating in the lower half of her body below the waist.</li><li><strong>Xylophone Resonance:</strong> When playing the xylophone, she feels the melody and sound waves traveling up the wooden mallets directly into her fingertips.</li><li><strong>Resonating Drums:</strong> By leaning against large bass drums, she absorbs the echoes and resonances flowing into her chest cavity and torso.</li><li><strong>Barefoot Performances:</strong> On wooden stages, she always removes her shoes and performs barefoot so that vibrations travel directly through her feet and move upward into her legs.</li><li><strong>Skin and Hair:</strong> Evelyn explains that sound literally tingles in her skin, her cheekbones, and even her hair roots, transforming her entire physical frame into an exquisitely attuned acoustic resonator.</li></ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Core thesis: Sensing vibrations through entire body rather than ears</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specific instruments: Drums (waist split), xylophone (fingertips via sticks)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Barefoot technique on wooden floors + somatic resonance (skin, hair, cheekbones)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">Part II: The Shehnai of Bismillah Khan — Exercise Q&A</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Emperor Aurangzeb ban the playing of the pungi? How is a shehnai different from a pungi?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Why Pungi was Banned:</strong><br>Emperor Aurangzeb banned the playing of the musical instrument <em>pungi</em> in the royal residence because it possessed a shrill, piercing, unpleasant, and unmelodious sound. It became generic name for reeded noisemakers.</p><p><strong>2. How Shehnai is Different from Pungi:</strong><br>A barber (<em>nai</em>) belonging to a family of professional musicians decided to improve the tonal quality of the pungi. He chose a natural hollow stem pipe that was longer and broader than the pungi and drilled seven holes into its body. When played by opening and closing some holes, it produced soft, melodious, and sweet notes. Since it was first played in the <em>Shah's</em> chambers by a <em>nai</em> (barber), it was named the <strong>shehnai</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Aurangzeb's ban: Shrill, harsh, unpleasant sound</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Shehnai construction: Longer, broader hollow pipe with 7 holes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Etymology: Played in Shah's chamber by a nai -> Shehnai</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where was the shehnai played traditionally? How did Bismillah Khan change this?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Traditional Use:</strong><br>Traditionally, the shehnai was played only as part of the <em>naubat</em> (traditional ensemble of nine instruments found at royal courts). It was strictly confined to temples and North Indian wedding rituals as an auspicious accompaniment.</p><p><strong>2. Bismillah Khan's Revolution:</strong><br>Ustad Bismillah Khan revolutionized the instrument by bringing the shehnai onto the classical concert stage. He invented new ragas and improvised melodies that were previously believed to be impossible on a reeded wind instrument, establishing the shehnai as an esteemed sovereign instrument of Indian classical music worldwide.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Traditional role: Part of royal naubat, temples, and weddings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bismillah Khan's transformation: Elevated to classical concert stage, invented ragas</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">When and how did Bismillah Khan get his big break? What happened on 15 August 1947?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Big Break (1938):</strong><br>Bismillah Khan received his historic national breakthrough with the opening of All India Radio in Lucknow in 1938. He soon became an often-heard and immensely popular shehnai broadcaster across the country.</p><p><strong>2. 15 August 1947 — Historic Day of Independence:</strong><br>On 15 August 1947, when India achieved independence, Bismillah Khan became the first Indian to greet the newly born nation with his shehnai from the historic ramparts of the Red Fort in Delhi. He poured his heart out into <em>Raag Kafi</em> before an audience that included Mahatma Gandhi and Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru (who subsequently delivered his famous 'Tryst with Destiny' speech).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">1938 Lucknow All India Radio opening</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">15 August 1947: First Indian to greet the independent nation from Red Fort</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Played Raag Kafi before Mahatma Gandhi and Pt. Nehru</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e2_7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e2_7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Bismillah Khan refuse to start a shehnai school in the USA? Find instances in the text showing his love for Benaras and Dumraon.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Refusal to Settle in the USA:</strong><br>One of Bismillah Khan's devoted students requested him to head a shehnai school in the United States and promised to recreate the entire atmosphere of Benaras by replicating the temples there. Bismillah Khan declined with polite humor, asking if the student would also be able to transport the Holy River Ganga to America. He emphasized that he could never live away from his motherland.</p><p><strong>2. Deep Love for Benaras and Dumraon:</strong><br>The text abounds with vivid instances showcasing his passionate attachment to his native roots:</p><ul><li><strong>Dumraon:</strong> He fondly remembered his childhood in Dumraon (Bihar), where he sang the Bhojpuri <em>'Chaita'</em> at the Bihariji temple and received a big 1.25 kg laddu as an award from the local Maharaja.</li><li><strong>River Ganga at Benaras:</strong> As a young apprentice, the flowing waters of the Ganga inspired him to improvise and invent new ragas on the quiet ghats of Varanasi and the temple of Mangala Maiya.</li><li><strong>Universal Patriotism:</strong> Bismillah Khan famously declared: <em>'Whenever I am in a foreign country, I keep yearning to see Hindustan. While in Mumbai, I think only of Benaras and the holy Ganga; and while in Benaras, I miss the unique mattha of Dumraon.'</em></li></ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Refusal rationale: Student couldn't transport River Ganga to America</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dumraon memories: Bihariji temple, Bhojpuri Chaita, Maharaja's laddu</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Benaras & Ganga: Solitude of ghats as musical inspiration + famous patriotic quote</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 2</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">HOTS / Inspiring Lives</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Comparative Analysis:</strong> Both Evelyn Glennie and Ustad Bismillah Khan achieved global excellence through unwavering dedication. What common character virtues enabled them to triumph?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Both Evelyn Glennie and Bismillah Khan embody passionate devotion (<em>riyaaz</em>), resilience, and absolute humility. Evelyn faced the catastrophic setback of profound deafness at age eleven, yet refused to abandon her dream, converting her whole body into an acoustic sensor. Bismillah Khan, despite attaining India's highest civilian honor—the Bharat Ratna—lived an austere, simple life dedicated entirely to music, rejecting commercial materialism and foreign citizenship. Both artists demonstrate that true mastery transcends physical limitations and material allure when propelled by pure love for one's craft.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Extract-Based Question</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Extract Analysis:</strong> <em>'Ustad Bismillah Khan's life is a perfect example of the rich, cultural heritage of India, one that effortlessly accepts that a devout Muslim like him can very naturally play the shehnai every morning at the Kashi Vishwanath temple.'</em><br>(a) What does this extract reveal about Indian secularism?<br>(b) Which temple did he play at every morning?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>(a)</strong> The extract illustrates the quintessential composite culture (<em>Ganga-Jamuni tehzeeb</em>) of India, where faith and art transcend communal divides, demonstrating harmonious coexistence and spiritual unity.<br><strong>(b)</strong> He played the shehnai every morning at the revered Kashi Vishwanath Temple in Varanasi.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(1)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "3": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch3\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">3</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 3: The Little Girl</h2>\n      <p>Katherine Mansfield — Kezia's Realization of Father's Love — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why was Kezia afraid of her father?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Kezia feared her father because he appeared massive, austere, and intimidating like a giant. His hands, neck, and mouth when he yawned seemed frighteningly large. He spoke to her in a loud, commanding tone, scrutinized her through his spectacles, and never played or smiled with her, making Kezia stammer nervously in his presence.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Physical intimidation: Giant-like appearance, huge hands and mouth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Strict behavior: Loud voice, sharp commands, no warmth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Psychological impact: Kezia stammered only before him</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who were the people in Kezia's family? What was her daily routine with her father?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Family Members:</strong> Kezia lived in a household consisting of four people: her father, her mother, her loving grandmother, and Kezia herself (along with Alice the cook).</p><p><strong>2. Daily Routine with Father:</strong> Every morning before leaving for office, her father entered her room and gave her a casual goodbye kiss, to which she answered politely: <em>'Goodbye, Father'</em>. In the evening, when he returned, he demanded his tea, newspaper, and slippers in a loud voice, and Kezia had to take off his boots in fearful silence.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Family members: Father, mother, grandmother, Kezia</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Morning routine: Casual goodbye kiss and polite response</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Evening routine: Demanding tea, newspaper, Kezia removing his boots</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In what ways did Kezia's grandmother encourage her to get to know her father better?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Kezia's grandmother consistently tried to bridge the emotional divide between Kezia and her parents. On Sunday afternoons, she encouraged Kezia to go downstairs to the drawing room to have a nice talk with her mother and father. Furthermore, when her father's birthday was approaching, the grandmother suggested that Kezia make a beautiful yellow silk pin-cushion as an affectionate surprise gift.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sunday afternoon visits to the drawing room for family talks</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Suggestion to stitch a birthday pin-cushion gift</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Kezia's efforts to please her father result in displeasing him very much?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>To craft the birthday pin-cushion, Kezia laboriously stitched three sides of fine yellow silk. Needing stuffing to fill the cushion, she searched her mother's bedroom and discovered sheets of fine paper on the bed table. Innocently unaware of their immense importance, Kezia tore them into tiny scraps and stuffed the cushion. Unfortunately, those papers contained her father's momentous speech for the Port Authority. When the loss was discovered, her father was infuriated and punished Kezia severely by beating her palms with a ruler.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Innocent mistake: Tearing sheets from mother's bedroom to stuff cushion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Importance: Contained father's crucial Port Authority speech</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Consequence: Father's fury and physical punishment with a ruler</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How was the MacDonalds' family different from Kezia's family?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The MacDonald family lived next door and presented a vivid, joyful contrast to Kezia's rigid home. Mr. MacDonald was warm, cheerful, and playfully affectionate. Kezia watched through the fence as he played 'tag' with his five children: baby Mao perched on his shoulders, two little girls hanging onto his coat pockets, running round and round flower beds, laughing continuously. Unlike Kezia's stern and distant father, Mr. MacDonald was accessible, patient, and full of fun.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrast of atmosphere: Fun-loving, joyful, interactive</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mr. MacDonald playing 'tag' with his 5 kids laughing together</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kezia's realization: There were 'different sorts of fathers'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — In-Depth Emotional Analysis (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e3_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e3_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Kezia's perception of her father change from a terrifying giant to a loving, hardworking human being? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Initial Fear and Estrangement:</strong><br>Initially, Kezia perceived her father as an authoritarian figure—cold, gigantic, impatient, and harsh. Her fear was reinforced when he beat her with a ruler for accidentally destroying his speech papers, despite her tearful explanation that it was intended as a birthday surprise.</p><p><strong>2. The Turning Point (Night of the Nightmare):</strong><br>One night, when both Kezia's mother and grandmother were away at the hospital, Kezia woke up screaming from a terrifying nightmare of a butcher with a knife and a rope. She expected to be alone and terrified, but to her astonishment, her father stood beside her bed holding a candle. He blew out the candle, scooped her gently into his strong arms, and carried her to his own large bedroom.</p><p><strong>3. Tenderness and Protection:</strong><br>He tucked her in carefully with warm blankets and lay down beside her. When Kezia still felt cold, he told her to rub her cold feet against his legs to get warm. Resting her head close beneath his armpit and clutching his shirt, Kezia listened to his beating heart.</p><p><strong>4. Epiphany and Deep Empathy:</strong><br>Kezia realized that her father worked excessively hard every day to provide for the family, leaving him too exhausted in the evenings to play like Mr. MacDonald. Her resentment melted into boundless compassion and love, concluding with her heartfelt murmur: <em>'What a big heart you've got, Father dear.'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Early stage: Fear of father as harsh, unloving giant + ruler punishment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Crisis: Nightmare during mother/grandmother's hospitalization</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Father's gentleness: Carrying her, tucking her in, warming her feet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Final realization: Hard work, fatigue, understanding his 'big heart'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 3</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Value-Based / Parenting</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Parent-Child Dynamics:</strong> Why is open communication crucial between parents and children? How does Katherine Mansfield illustrate this through Kezia and her father?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Mansfield illustrates that authoritarian parenting without verbal affection creates profound emotional distance and unnecessary trauma. Kezia's father genuinely loved his daughter, but his gruff demeanor, exhausted silence, and harsh discipline made Kezia view him as a monster. Had he communicated his fatigue gently or explained the significance of his work calmly, Kezia would not have suffered from fear and stuttering. The story underscores that parents must express warmth visibly, and children need an emotionally safe environment where mistakes are corrected with guidance rather than corporal punishment.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Analysis:</strong> Write a character sketch of Kezia's grandmother.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Kezia's grandmother is the gentle emotional pillar of the household. She possesses deep understanding, patience, and empathetic wisdom. Unlike Kezia's parents who are preoccupied with social standing and business, grandmother provides Kezia with unconditional love, comforting lap, and emotional solace. She actively attempts to cultivate filial affection by sending Kezia to converse with her parents on Sundays and suggesting the thoughtful birthday cushion. When Kezia is weeping after the ruler beating, grandmother wraps her in a shawl and rocks her in the rocking chair, assuring her that pain will pass with sleep.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "4": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch4\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">4</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 4: A Truly Beautiful Mind</h2>\n      <p>Albert Einstein — Genius Scientist and World Citizen — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Einstein's mother think he was a freak? What was unusual about his childhood speech?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Einstein's mother considered him a 'freak' because his head seemed abnormally and unusually large. Furthermore, Albert did not speak at all until the age of two-and-a-half. Even when he finally began talking, he uttered everything twice in a hesitant, repetitive manner. Because he did not know what to do with other children, his playmates mocked him as 'Brother Boring'.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mother's perception: Abnormally large head</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Speech delay: Did not speak until 2½ years</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Peculiarity: Uttered everything twice; dubbed 'Brother Boring'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did a school headmaster once say about Einstein? What instrument did Einstein learn to play?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>A headmaster once told Einstein's father that whatever Albert chose as a profession would never matter because <em>'he'll never make a success at anything.'</em> Despite this discouraging prediction, Einstein learned to play the violin at his mother's insistence at age six, becoming a gifted amateur violinist throughout his life.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Headmaster's verdict: Never succeed at anything</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Violin mastery: Began at age 6, gifted amateur for life</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Einstein leave the school in Munich for good?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Einstein left the high school in Munich at age fifteen because he detested the school's suffocating regimentation, strict discipline, and authoritarian teachers. The mechanical learning environment stifled his creative thinking, and he felt so stifled that he left the institution permanently to study in liberal Switzerland.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reasons: Suffocating regimentation, strict authoritarian discipline</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Felt stifled and moved to liberal, open-minded Switzerland</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Einstein want to study in Switzerland rather than in Munich? Who was Mileva Maric?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Preference for Switzerland:</strong> Einstein preferred to continue his education in German-speaking Switzerland (University of Zurich) because the academic atmosphere was significantly more liberal, tolerant, and open to individual reasoning than rigid Munich.</p><p><strong>2. Mileva Maric:</strong> Mileva Maric was a young Serbian student whom Einstein met at Zurich University. He found her to be a 'clever creature' who shared his passion for science and literature and despised the philistines (unintellectual people). They fell in love and later married.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Switzerland's liberal, open-minded academic culture</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mileva Maric: Serbian fellow student, intellectual companion, later wife</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did Einstein call his desk drawer at the patent office in Bern? Why?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>While working as a technical expert at the patent office in Bern, Einstein jokingly referred to his desk drawer as the <strong>'Bureau of Theoretical Physics'</strong>. This was because while officially assessing other inventors' patent applications, he secretly worked on his own pathbreaking theoretical ideas in physics and kept his secret research notes locked inside that drawer.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Name: 'Bureau of Theoretical Physics'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason: Developing his own groundbreaking physics theories in secret</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did Einstein write a letter to Franklin D. Roosevelt in 1939? What was its impact?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Reason for Letter:</strong> When the Nazis came to power in Germany and discovered nuclear fission in Berlin, American scientists were terrified that Hitler would construct an atomic bomb. Urged by colleagues, Einstein wrote to US President Franklin D. Roosevelt on 2 August 1939, warning him that a single bomb of this type could destroy an entire port along with surrounding territory.</p><p><strong>2. Impact:</strong> The letter prompted the American government to launch the secret 'Manhattan Project', which culminated in the development and dropping of atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Warning about Nazi Germany building atomic bomb via nuclear fission</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Impact: US launched Manhattan Project, leading to atomic bomb creation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Global Legacy and Scientific Humanism (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e4_7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e4_7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the chapter titled 'A Truly Beautiful Mind'? How did Einstein's scientific genius merge with his advocacy for world peace? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Significance of the Title:</strong><br>The title <em>'A Truly Beautiful Mind'</em> highlights that Albert Einstein was not merely a brilliant intellectual giant in mathematics and physics, but also a profoundly compassionate human being possessing a noble, philanthropic soul dedicated to global harmony.</p><p><strong>2. Agony over Hiroshima and Nagasaki:</strong><br>When the United States dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, Einstein was deeply devastated by the catastrophic loss of human life. In response, he wrote a passionate public missive to the United Nations proposing the formation of a unified <strong>World Government</strong> to prevent international military destruction.</p><p><strong>3. Devotion to World Peace and Democracy:</strong><br>Unlike scientists who divorced morality from science, Einstein spent his later decades actively campaigning against atomic weapons, advocating nuclear disarmament, promoting social justice, and championing individual freedom. When he passed away in 1955 at age 76, he was celebrated not merely as the discoverer of Relativity, but as a beloved <strong>world citizen and champion of humanity</strong>. His mind was truly 'beautiful' because his genius served life, peace, and universal brotherhood.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Title justification: Synthesis of intellectual genius and humanitarian empathy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Response to atomic bombings: Devastation and UN proposal for World Government</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Later activism: Disarmament campaign, anti-war crusade, world peace</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Legacy: Celebrated as a 'world citizen' who harmonized science with ethics</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 4</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Competency-Based / Scientific Ethics</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Science and Morality:</strong> <em>'Einstein was deeply shaken by the extent of the destruction.'</em> How does Einstein's reaction to the atomic bomb reflect the moral responsibility of scientists?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Einstein's profound grief demonstrates that scientific discovery must never be detached from ethical responsibility. Although his famous equation E = mc² laid the theoretical foundation for nuclear energy, he was horrified when politicians weaponized his insight into an instrument of mass slaughter. Einstein actively utilized his global fame to campaign for disarmament and peaceful co-existence. His life reminds contemporary researchers that scientific advancement without moral consciousness leads to annihilation, and scientists bear an ongoing duty to protect humanity from the misuse of their inventions.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">HOTS / Milestone Analysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Analytical Thinking:</strong> Explain the significance of the year 1905 and the 1919 solar eclipse in Einstein's life.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The year 1905 is known as Einstein's <em>'Annus Mirabilis'</em> (Miracle Year), during which the 26-year-old patent clerk published four revolutionary papers on light quanta, Brownian motion, electrodynamics, and the Special Theory of Relativity containing E = mc². In 1915, he published his General Theory of Relativity. The solar eclipse of 1919 provided empirical proof that gravity bends light rays from distant stars, precisely as Einstein had mathematically calculated. Newspapers across the world hailed his work as a 'Scientific Revolution', transforming him overnight into an international legend.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "5": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch5\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">5</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 5: The Snake and the Mirror</h2>\n      <p>Vaikom Muhammad Basheer — A Tale of Vanity, Fear, and Irony — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What was the doctor's financial condition when the incident took place? Describe his room.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The doctor was a homeopathic practitioner who had just started his medical practice; consequently, his earnings were meager. He had merely sixty rupees in his suitcase, along with a few shirts, dhotis, and one solitary black coat. His rented room was a small, electrified-less outer room with tiled roof supported by gables where rats ran continuously back and forth across the rafters.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Financial condition: Meager earnings, just 60 rupees in cash</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Belongings: Few shirts, dhotis, one solitary black coat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Room description: Small rented outer room, no electricity, infested with rats</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What two 'important' and 'earth-shaking' decisions did the doctor make while looking in the mirror?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>While admiring his reflection in the large mirror by kerosene lamp light, the vain young bachelor made two comic decisions:</p><ol><li><strong>Important Decision:</strong> He decided to shave daily and grow a thin mustache to look more handsome.</li><li><strong>Earth-Shaking Decision:</strong> He decided to always keep an attractive, captivating smile on his face to appear charming to everyone.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">First decision: Shave daily and grow a thin mustache</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Second decision: Always keep an attractive smile on his face</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What kind of wife did the doctor wish to marry, and what kind of wife did he actually get?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Dream Wife:</strong> The doctor wished to marry a wealthy woman doctor with a flourishing medical practice. Crucially, he wanted her to be stout (fat) so that if he ever committed a foolish mistake, she would not be able to run fast and catch him!</p><p><strong>2. Real Wife:</strong> In reality, his destiny was entirely the opposite: his wife was a thin, reed-like person with the athletic gifts of a sprinter who could run very fast.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dream wife: Wealthy, fat doctor who couldn't chase him</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Real wife: Thin, slender sprinter with great running speed</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did the snake land on the doctor? What was his immediate physical reaction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>While the doctor sat lost in admiring thoughts before the mirror, a dull thud occurred followed by a cold, slithering sound. Before he could turn his head, a fat cobra wriggled over the back of his chair and landed heavily on his shoulder, rapidly coiling around his left arm above the elbow with its hood spread merely three or four inches from his face.</p><p>The doctor was paralyzed with terror. He did not jump, cry out, or tremble. He sat petrified like a stone image in flesh, holding his breath completely.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Landing: Slithered over chair back, coiled around left arm above elbow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reaction: Turned into a stone image in flesh, no movement, holding breath</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why did the snake leave the doctor's arm? What humorous observation did the doctor make?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The cobra slowly turned its head and caught sight of its own reflection in the mirror on the table. Entranced by what it saw, the snake uncoiled from the doctor's arm and crept onto the table, moving closer to the mirror. The doctor humorously wondered whether it was a male or female snake, whether it was admiring its own beauty, or contemplating wearing eye shadow, mascara, or a vermilion spot on its forehead!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Snake fascinated by its reflection in the mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uncoiled and slithered onto the table towards mirror</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Humorous thought: Snake admiring beauty or wearing makeup/vermilion spot</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Humor, Irony, and Vanity (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e5_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e5_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the story 'The Snake and the Mirror' skillfully contrast human vanity with mortal terror? Discuss with examples. (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Human Vanity and Narcissism:</strong><br>At the opening of the story, the young homeopathic doctor is deeply consumed by self-admiration. Living in poverty in a shabby rat-infested room, he nevertheless sits before the mirror, obsessively adjusting his hair parting, smiling broadly, and resolving to grow a mustache to amplify his bachelor charm. He feels proud of being a handsome doctor and indulges in laughable fantasies about marrying a rich fat woman.</p><p><strong>2. Sudden Confrontation with Death:</strong><br>His bloated vanity is abruptly punctured when a venomous cobra drops onto his shoulder and coils around his arm. In an instant, his arrogant pride evaporates. He feels completely helpless, realizing that death is hovering barely four inches away. Stripped of his medical arrogance, he acknowledges his weakness, calling himself a <em>'poor, foolish and stupid doctor'</em> who did not even keep anti-venom medicine in his room.</p><p><strong>3. Ironic Resolution:</strong><br>The humor peaks when the snake, too, exhibits vanity—abandoning the doctor to admire its own reflection in the mirror, allowing the terrified doctor to sprint for his life into the night.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Vanity depicted: Admiring reflection, foolish marriage fantasies, grooming plans</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mortal terror: Cobra's deadly coil, realization of utter helplessness and mortality</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Humorous irony: Vanity of the snake mirroring human vanity + doctor's flight</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 5</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Humor & Irony</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> What role does the mirror play as a central motif in the story?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The mirror serves as the crucial literary pivot of the narrative. In the first half, it acts as a catalyst for human vanity, encouraging the young doctor to indulge in narcissistic daydreams and superficial plans. In the second half, the mirror becomes an instrument of salvation. When the deadly cobra looks into the mirror, it is lured away by its own image, saving the doctor's life without a single drop of blood spilled. The mirror thus reflects both human vanity and animal curiosity, binding tension and comedy together.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Narrative Voice:</strong> How does Vaikom Muhammad Basheer employ self-deprecating humor through the doctor's narrative?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The doctor recounts his terrifying ordeal with delightful self-mockery. Instead of portraying himself as a heroic survivor, he openly confesses his vanity, his poverty, and his cowardice. He chuckles at his sixty rupees, his lone black coat, and his ridiculous thoughts about marrying a fat woman. When facing the snake, he candidly admits that he felt like a foolish simpleton who had no medicines in his room. Even in the aftermath, when thieves ransack his room and leave behind his dirty vest as a final insult, the doctor laughs along with his listeners, highlighting his warm, cheerful acceptance of life's absurdities.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "6": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch6\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">6</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 6: My Childhood</h2>\n      <p>Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam — Values, Secular Harmony & Upbringing — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where was Abdul Kalam's house? What characteristics does he say he inherited from his parents?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. House Location:</strong> Kalam's ancestral house was located on the Mosque Street in the island town of Rameswaram (in erstwhile Madras State). It was a fairly large pucca house built of limestone and brick in the middle of the nineteenth century.</p><p><strong>2. Inherited Values:</strong> From his father, Jainulabdeen, Kalam inherited <em>honesty and self-discipline</em>. From his mother, Ashiamma, he inherited <em>faith in goodness and deep kindness</em>, as well as an expansive generosity of spirit.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Location: Mosque Street, Rameswaram</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">From Father: Honesty and self-discipline</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">From Mother: Faith in goodness and deep kindness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who were Abdul Kalam's school friends? What did they later become?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Kalam had three close childhood friends from orthodox Hindu Brahmin families:</p><ol><li><strong>Ramanadha Sastry:</strong> Son of Pakshi Lakshmana Sastry (head priest of the Rameswaram Temple). He later took over the priesthood of the famous Rameswaram temple from his father.</li><li><strong>Aravindan:</strong> Entered the business of arranging transport and logistics for visiting Hindu pilgrims.</li><li><strong>Sivaprakasan:</strong> Became a prominent catering contractor for the Southern Railways.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ramanadha Sastry -> Head priest of Rameswaram temple</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Aravindan -> Transport contractor for pilgrims</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sivaprakasan -> Catering contractor for Southern Railways</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Abdul Kalam earn his first wages? How did he feel about it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When World War II broke out in 1939, emergency was declared and the train halt at Rameswaram station was suspended. Newspaper bundles thrown from the moving train between Rameswaram and Dhanuskodi needed to be collected. Kalam's cousin Samsuddin, who distributed newspapers, engaged Kalam to catch the bundles. For this work, Kalam earned his first wages. Even half a century later, Kalam recalled that he felt an immense surge of personal pride in earning his own money for the very first time.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Context: WWII emergency, train halt cancelled at Rameswaram</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Task: Helping cousin Samsuddin catch newspaper bundles from moving trains</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Emotional impact: Tremendous surge of pride in earning first wages</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What happened when a new teacher arrived at Rameswaram Elementary School? How was it resolved?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When Kalam was in fifth standard, a new teacher arrived who could not stomach a Hindu priest's son (Ramanadha Sastry) sitting beside a Muslim boy wearing a cap. He ordered Kalam to go and sit on the back bench. Both boys felt deeply dejected and wept. Ramanadha reported the humiliation to his father, Pakshi Lakshmana Sastry.</p><p>Lakshmana Sastry immediately summoned the teacher and bluntly reprimanded him, telling him not to spread the poison of social inequality and communal intolerance in the minds of innocent children. He gave the teacher the choice to apologize or leave the school and island. The teacher repented, reformed, and learned a lasting lesson in communal harmony.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prejudice: New teacher segregated Kalam to back bench due to cap/religion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Intervention: Pakshi Lakshmana Sastry summoned and reprimanded teacher</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Resolution: Teacher reformed; island's secular unity upheld</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Sivasubramania Iyer's wife react when Kalam was first invited for dinner? How did she change on his second visit?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. First Visit:</strong> Science teacher Sivasubramania Iyer, a rebel Brahmin, invited Kalam home for a meal. His conservative wife was horrified at the idea of a Muslim boy dining in her ritually pure kitchen and adamantly refused to serve him. Iyer did not lose his temper; he served Kalam with his own hands and sat beside him to eat.</p><p><strong>2. Second Visit:</strong> The following weekend, when Kalam revisited, Iyer's wife took him inside her kitchen herself and served him food with her own hands, having observed his cultured manners and overcome her communal prejudice.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">First visit: Wife refused to serve Muslim boy in her kitchen; teacher served him</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Second visit: Wife reformed, welcomed Kalam inside kitchen, served him herself</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Communal Harmony and Parental Wisdom (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e6_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e6_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did Kalam's father say when Kalam asked permission to leave Rameswaram to study at Ramanathapuram? Discuss the profound wisdom of Jainulabdeen's response. (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Father's Philosophical Blessing:</strong><br>When Kalam sought permission to pursue higher education at the district headquarters in Ramanathapuram, his father Jainulabdeen encouraged him without hesitation, stating: <em>'Abul! I know you have to go away to grow. Does the seagull not fly across the sun, alone and without a nest?'</em></p><p><strong>2. Consoling the Hesitant Mother:</strong><br>Kalam's mother was hesitant and tearful about sending her young son away. To console her, Jainulabdeen quoted the Lebanese poet-philosopher <strong>Khalil Gibran</strong>:</p><blockquote><em>'Your children are not your children. They are the sons and daughters of Life's longing for itself. They come through you but not from you, and though they are with you yet they belong not to you. You may give them your love but not your thoughts, for they have their own thoughts.'</em></blockquote><p><strong>3. Significance:</strong><br>This response highlights Jainulabdeen's extraordinary progressive wisdom. Despite possessing no formal schooling, he understood that parental love must foster independence rather than possessive attachment. He granted Kalam the freedom to soar beyond the horizons of Rameswaram, instilling wings of self-reliance that eventually made him India's 'Missile Man' and President.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Father's seagull metaphor: Must fly alone across the sun to grow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Quoting Khalil Gibran on children belonging to the future, not parents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Philosophical depth: Progressive mindset, selfless parenting, nurturing freedom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 6</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Value-Based / Secularism</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Theme of Social Harmony:</strong> How does Kalam's childhood demonstrate that communal harmony is built through individual courage and enlightened leadership?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Kalam's upbringing in Rameswaram proves that communal peace requires proactive defense. When religious bigotry threatened the town through the new schoolteacher, the high priest Lakshmana Sastry directly confronted the perpetrator, demanding reformation rather than appeasement. Similarly, science teacher Sivasubramania Iyer challenged domestic orthodoxy by serving Kalam himself, transforming his wife's rigid heart through patient example. These episodes show that societal prejudice cannot survive when respected elders and teachers champion equality, proving that India's unity relies on active empathy and moral courage.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Analysis:</strong> Write a character sketch of Sivasubramania Iyer.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Sivasubramania Iyer was an orthodox Brahmin science teacher who acted as a radical catalyst for social transformation. He was a visionary who believed that merit transcends caste and religion. When his conservative wife refused to serve Kalam, Iyer remained composed, avoiding domestic conflict while firmly standing by his convictions. He broke caste taboos by dining beside Kalam and mentoring him: <em>'Once you decide to change the system, such problems have to be confronted.'</em> His broadminded mentorship provided Kalam with intellectual ambition and secular values.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "7": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch7\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">7</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 7: Reach for the Top</h2>\n      <p>Part I: Santosh Yadav | Part II: Maria Sharapova — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">Part I: Santosh Yadav — Exercise Q&A</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why was the holy man who gave Santosh's mother his blessings surprised? What does the name 'Santosh' mean?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Holy Man's Surprise:</strong> In the traditional patriarchal village society of Haryana, the birth of a son was considered a blessing while a daughter was not welcomed. A visiting wandering holy man assumed the family wanted a boy. However, Santosh's grandmother requested blessings for a daughter, astonishing the holy man who readily granted the unconventional blessing.</p><p><strong>2. Meaning of 'Santosh':</strong> The girl was named <strong>'Santosh'</strong>, which means <em>contentment</em>. Yet Santosh was not always content with traditional societal limits placed on women.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tradition expected prayer for a son; grandmother requested daughter</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Name 'Santosh' means contentment; ironic given her rebellious spirit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Santosh show her independent spirit regarding clothing and education at an early age?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Clothing:</strong> While other girls in her village wore traditional Indian dresses, Santosh insisted on wearing shorts, declaring that she preferred rational and comfortable clothing.</p><p><strong>2. Education:</strong> At age sixteen, when girls in her village were pressured into marriage, Santosh adamantly refused, threatening her parents that she would never marry unless she received a proper education. When her parents refused to pay her fees at a Delhi school, she calmly informed them that she would fund her own schooling by working part-time. Impressed by her determination, her father agreed to pay for her education.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clothing: Wore shorts instead of traditional attire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Marriage refusal at age 16 demanding education</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Self-reliance: Offered to work part-time to pay Delhi school fees</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Santosh Yadav take up mountaineering? Describe her training at Uttarkashi.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>While studying at Maharani College in Jaipur, Santosh's hostel room faced the Aravalli Hills. She used to watch villagers climb up the hills and vanish over the crest. Curious, she went there herself one day and found a few mountaineers. They encouraged her to join them.</p><p>Santosh saved money, enrolled without her parents' permission in the Nehru Institute of Mountaineering at Uttarkashi, and wrote an apology letter to her father. She underwent rigorous expeditions every year, developing extraordinary physical endurance, resistance to extreme cold and altitude, and an iron will.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Discovery: Watching climbers on Aravalli Hills from Jaipur hostel room</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Enrolled at Nehru Institute of Mountaineering at Uttarkashi</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Developed physical stamina, cold resistance, and iron determination</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What unique mountaineering records did Santosh Yadav set? What incident showed her deep humanity?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Global Mountaineering Records:</strong><br>In 1992, at barely twenty years of age, Santosh Yadav scaled Mount Everest, becoming the <strong>youngest woman in the world</strong> to achieve the feat. Within twelve months, she joined an Indo-Nepalese Women's Expedition and scaled Everest a <strong>second time</strong>, becoming the only woman in mountaineering history to conquer Mount Everest twice.</p><p><strong>2. Exemplary Humanity on the Slopes:</strong><br>During her 1992 Everest expedition, Santosh displayed extraordinary compassion. At the South Col, she desperately tried to save a dying climber, though she was unsuccessful. However, she successfully saved the life of another teammate, <strong>Mohan Singh</strong>, by selflessly sharing her own precious oxygen bottle with him—a sacrifice without which Mohan Singh would have certainly perished.</p><p><strong>3. Environmental Commitment:</strong><br>Santosh was also an ardent environmentalist who collected and brought down 500 kilograms of garbage from the fragile slopes of the Himalayas.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Record 1: Youngest woman to scale Everest (age 20 in 1992)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Record 2: Only woman in the world to scale Everest twice</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Humanity: Shared oxygen to save Mohan Singh at the South Col</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Environmentalist: Brought down 500 kg garbage from Himalayas</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">Part II: Maria Sharapova — Exercise Q&A</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What sacrifices did young Maria Sharapova make to train in Florida? How did she handle bullying?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Heartbreaking Sacrifices:</strong> At barely nine years old, Maria was separated from her mother, Yelena, who was forced to stay back in Siberia due to visa restrictions. Maria traveled to Florida, USA, with her father Yuri, who worked grueling shifts to fund her tennis training. Maria endured profound homesickness and loneliness for two long years.</p><p><strong>2. Handling Bullying:</strong> Because Maria went to bed at 8 PM, older pupils returned at 11 PM, woke her up, and ordered her to clean the room. Instead of breaking down into tears, Maria became more quietly determined, tough, and mentally resilient. She learned how to take care of herself and never considered quitting.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Separation from mother at age 9; father working relentless shifts in Florida</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bullying by older pupils turned into mental toughness and determination</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e7_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e7_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are Maria Sharapova's hobbies? What is her attitude towards her Russian nationality?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Hobbies and Fashion:</strong> Maria loves fashion, singing, dancing, reading Arthur Conan Doyle's detective novels (Sherlock Holmes), and has a fondness for sophisticated evening gowns coupled with pancakes with chocolate spread and fizzy orange drinks.</p><p><strong>2. Russian Nationality:</strong> Though she has lived in the United States since age seven and speaks with an American accent, Maria proudly asserts her Russian citizenship: <em>'My blood is totally Russian. I will play the Olympics for Russia if they want me.'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hobbies: Fashion, singing, Sherlock Holmes novels, evening gowns, pancakes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">National pride: Proud of Russian roots, holds Russian passport, Olympic allegiance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 7</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Comparative Character Study</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Determination & Resilience:</strong> Compare the life journeys of Santosh Yadav and Maria Sharapova. What common message do they offer young women?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Both Santosh Yadav and Maria Sharapova achieved pinnacle global success through unyielding grit, sacrifices, and refusal to conform to societal handicaps. Santosh defied rural patriarchal expectations in Haryana, resisted child marriage, and conquered Mount Everest twice. Maria endured painful maternal separation at age nine in Siberia, worked through poverty in Florida, and overcame locker-room bullying to capture Wimbledon at seventeen. Both women demonstrate that unwavering focus, mental toughness, and hard work can overcome gender bias, poverty, and isolation, proving that excellence is earned through persistent struggle.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Extract-Based Question</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Patriotism:</strong> <em>'Then I unfurled the Indian tricolour and held it aloft on the roof of the world... It was truly a spiritual moment. I felt proud as an Indian.'</em><br>(a) Who is the speaker?<br>(b) What was the emotional impact of hoisting the flag on Mount Everest?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>(a)</strong> The speaker is Indian mountaineer <strong>Santosh Yadav</strong>.<br><strong>(b)</strong> Hoisting the Indian tricolour on top of Mount Everest was an overwhelming, spiritual experience that filled her with profound national pride, marking India's triumphant presence on the highest peak of the earth.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "8": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch8\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">8</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 8: Kathmandu</h2>\n      <p>Vikram Seth — Pashupatinath, Baudhnath & The Flute Player — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e8_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e8_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Contrast the atmosphere at Pashupatinath temple with that at the Baudhnath stupa.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Pashupatinath Temple (Feverish Chaos):</strong><br>The Hindu shrine of Pashupatinath exists in an atmosphere of <em>'febrile confusion'</em>. Priests, hawkers, devotees, tourists, cows, monkeys, and pigeons roam the grounds. Devotees jostle and elbow each other for entrance; a royal princess arrives causing bowing; monkeys fight and leap over shivalingas; and cremations take place along the sacred Bagmati river amidst washerwomen and trash.</p><p><strong>2. Baudhnath Stupa (Immaculate Serenity):</strong><br>In stark contrast, the Buddhist stupa of Baudhnath offers a haven of profound silence, peace, and stillness. Its immense white dome is ringed by a quiet road lined with small Tibetan shops selling felt bags and silver jewelry. There are no crowds, noise, or pushiness; it radiates an oasis of calm detachment.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pashupatinath: Febrile confusion, crowds, monkeys, jostling, cremations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Baudhnath: Immense white dome, Tibetan shops, profound silence and calm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e8_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e8_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What sign is proclaimed at the entrance of Pashupatinath? What humorous incident occurred with Western tourists?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>At the entrance of Pashupatinath temple, a sign strictly proclaims: <strong>'Entrance for the Hindus only'</strong>. A humorous scene ensues when a party of Western tourists dressed in saffron dhotis struggle to convince a temple guard that they are genuine Hindus. The skeptical policeman remains unconvinced and refuses them entry because they do not look authentically Hindu.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Signboard: 'Entrance for the Hindus only'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Humorous incident: Saffron-clad Western tourists barred by skeptical guard</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e8_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e8_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the fight between the two monkeys at Pashupatinath.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Two monkeys engage in a furious duel on the temple grounds. One chases the other, who leaps onto a holy Shivalinga, then runs screaming around the temples, down toward the holy river Bagmati. A monkey jumps atop a temple roof and finally scrambles away, creating commotion amid the solemnity of rituals and cremations.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Duel between two monkeys jumping onto Shivalinga</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chasing and screeching around temples down to River Bagmati</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e8_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e8_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does Vikram Seth describe the flute seller in Kathmandu? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Appearance and Setup:</strong><br>Near his hotel, Vikram Seth observes a flute seller standing in the corner of a square. In his hand, he holds a pole from which protrudes an attachment carrying fifty or sixty bamboo flutes in all directions, resembling the quills of a porcupine. The instruments are classical bansuris, reed flutes, and recorders.</p><p><strong>2. Unique Demeanor:</strong><br>Unlike aggressive street hawkers who yell loudly to attract customers, the flute seller does not shout or pester people. He places the pole on the ground, selects a flute, and plays meditatively, slowly, and without excessive display. His music rises clearly above the din of traffic and hawkers' cries.</p><p><strong>3. Casual Business:</strong><br>Occasionally he makes a sale, but in an offhanded, casual manner, as if selling flutes were merely incidental to his artistic pleasure. Then he returns to playing his flute with quiet absorption.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Flute pole resembling porcupine quills with 50-60 flutes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Quiet, meditative playing without shouting or aggressive marketing</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Offhand attitude: Flute playing was his art, sales were incidental</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Philosophical Reflection on the Flute (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e8_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e8_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does Vikram Seth find the music of the flute so universally compelling and deeply moving? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Universal Presence of the Flute:</strong><br>Vikram Seth observes that the flute is the most universal and common musical instrument across all human cultures. Every society has its own variant: the reed neh of the Middle East, the cross-flutes of Japan, the high-pitched folk flutes of South America, the classical bansuri of India, and the European recorder. While each differs in finger-holes, compass, and fingering, all flutes share an identical essence.</p><p><strong>2. The Flute as Metaphor for Human Life:</strong><br>Seth reflects that the flute operates through the most fundamental human function: <strong>the breath of life</strong>. To produce music, a human must breathe into the instrument, pausing periodically to inhale. The music pauses and resumes just as human existence pulses with each living breath.</p><p><strong>3. Emotional Resonance:</strong><br>Listening to the Kathmandu bansuri, Seth finds himself transported. His awareness shifts from superficial travel impressions to the profound oneness of all human beings, united by the universal language of breath and melody.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Universal existence: Middle Eastern neh, Japanese shakuhachi, Indian bansuri</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Breath metaphor: Living breath woven into melody, reflecting common humanity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Philosophical realization: Universal acoustic bridge connecting all cultures</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 8</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Sensory Imagery & Style</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Craft:</strong> How does Vikram Seth use vivid sensory imagery to bring the streets of Kathmandu alive?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Seth engages all five senses to create a vibrant mosaic of Kathmandu. Visually, he juxtaposes the chaotic crowds and flower-strewn deities of Pashupatinath with the immaculate white dome of Baudhnath. Auditory details abound—blaring car horns, bicycle bells, Hindi film songs from radios, and the serene notes of the bansuri. Smells of incense smoke mingle with exhaust fumes and cremations by the river, while tastes of roasted corn and marzipan sweets evoke street life. Through precise verbs and lively adjectives, Seth immerses the reader into the living texture of the city.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Value-Based Reflection</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Spiritual Contrasts:</strong> What philosophical insight emerges from comparing Pashupatinath with Baudhnath?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Pashupatinath and Baudhnath represent two complementary facets of Eastern spirituality. Pashupatinath reflects the passionate, colorful, and noisy engagement with worldly life—devotion, trade, animals, and death existing side by side. Baudhnath embodies Buddhist tranquility, detachment, and peaceful contemplation. Together, they show that spirituality embraces both the turbulent storm of everyday existence and the still, silent harbor of inner peace.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "9": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch9\" data-book=\"Beehive Prose\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">9</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE PROSE</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 9: If I Were You</h2>\n      <p>Douglas James — Play of Wits between Gerrard & Intruder — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who is Gerrard? What was he doing when the Intruder broke into his cottage?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Gerrard is a cultured playwright and theatre artist who lives in a secluded cottage in the wilds of Essex. When the Intruder broke in with a revolver, Gerrard was speaking on the telephone making arrangements for props, and packing a travel bag for a theatrical rehearsal.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identity: Cultured playwright and theatre artist living in Essex</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Activity: Speaking on telephone and packing travel bag with theatrical props</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why had the Intruder broken into Gerrard's cottage? What was his sinister plan?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The Intruder was a fugitive criminal who had murdered a policeman and was on the run. Because he closely resembled Gerrard in physique and stature, his plan was to murder Gerrard and steal his identity, car, and clothes, thereby living comfortably as 'Vincent Charles Gerrard' without fear of the police.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identity: Jewel thief and cop-killer on the run</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sinister plan: Murder Gerrard and assume his identity to evade hanging</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Gerrard keep his cool when threatened with a revolver?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Despite facing an armed criminal ready to shoot, Gerrard never panicked. He maintained remarkable composure, adopted a witty, humorous, and theatrical tone, engaged the Intruder in conversation, and flattered the criminal's ego, giving himself time to fabricate an ingenious escape plan.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">No panic; witty, nonchalant, and theatrical composure</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Engaged Intruder in conversation to formulate counter-strategy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What clever story did Gerrard invent to deceive the Intruder?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Gerrard fabricated a brilliant tale, convincing the Intruder that he himself was a dangerous criminal and murderer on the run. He explained that his mysterious habits, telephone orders, and sudden disappearances were because the police were hunting him. As proof, he pointed to his packed bag, disguises (disguise outfit, false moustaches), and warned the Intruder that if he impersonated Gerrard, he would be hanged anyway!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Invented identity: Claimed he was also a criminal and killer on the run</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Evidence cited: Packed bag, false moustaches, disguises, mysterious habits</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Trap: Warned Intruder that taking Gerrard's persona guarantees hanging</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Gerrard trap the Intruder? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Gerrard told the Intruder that his accomplice was stationed at the road corner to signal if the police arrived. When the telephone rang, Gerrard claimed it was the warning signal. He urged the Intruder to escape with him in his car through the garage. Gerrard opened a door, claiming it led straight to the garage. When the Intruder leaned forward to inspect it with his revolver ready, Gerrard gave him a sudden, forceful push into the dark cupboard, knocked the revolver out of his hand, slammed the door shut, and locked him securely inside. He then picked up the phone to call the police.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deception: Pretended cupboard door was exit to garage for escape</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Action: Pushed Intruder inside, knocked away revolver, locked door</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Resolution: Picked up phone to summon police and use event for his next play</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Wit vs Brute Force (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"e9_6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('e9_6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the play 'If I Were You' establish that presence of mind and intellect triumph over brute force and violence? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Power Asymmetry:</strong><br>The play pits a dangerous, gun-toting criminal against an unarmed playwright. The Intruder represents brute force, ruthlessness, and arrogant malice, believing his revolver gives him absolute dominance over Gerrard's life.</p><p><strong>2. Gerrard's Intellectual Strategy:</strong><br>Instead of groveling or crying, Gerrard weaponizes his dramatic intellect and psychological acumen. He recognizes the Intruder's foolish vanity and manipulates his gullibility. By maintaining an air of witty nonchalance, Gerrard disarms the criminal mentally. He invents an airtight backstory claiming he is also a murderer on the run, backing his claim with genuine theatrical props (disguise clothes and false mustaches).</p><p><strong>3. Decisive Climax:</strong><br>Gerrard orchestrates a brilliant trap, tricking the Intruder into inspecting a cupboard disguised as a garage exit. With a swift push and slammed door, intellect cleanly neutralizes violence without firing a single bullet. The play delivers a timeless lesson: cool presence of mind, wit, and composure in a crisis will always triumph over blind aggression.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrast: Brute force of armed criminal vs intellectual wit of playwright</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Psychological manipulation: Feigned nonchalance, leveraging theatrical props</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Climax & Moral: Outsmarting violence without shed blood; triumph of presence of mind</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 9</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Dramatic Irony</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> How does Douglas James utilize dramatic irony and theatrical elements in 'If I Were You'?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The play is soaked in rich theatrical irony. Gerrard is an actual playwright whose daily life revolves around stagecraft, disguises, and melodrama. When confronted by a real-life villain, Gerrard simply casts the criminal into an impromptu drama. The false moustaches and disguise kit—meant for a theatrical production—serve as authenticating 'proof' of his criminal persona. The Intruder, thinking he is the director of the crime, becomes a hapless puppet in Gerrard's play, culminating in the ironic twist where Gerrard promises to put the entire episode into his next stage play.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Analysis:</strong> Write a character sketch of Gerrard.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Vincent Charles Gerrard is a refined, quick-witted, and extraordinarily courageous playwright. He possesses sharp observational powers, dry British humor, and iron nerves. Even when staring down the barrel of a loaded revolver, he banters playfully, refusing to show terror. Gerrard is a master of psychology; he quickly detects the Intruder's intellectual limitations and crafts a spontaneous fiction to deceive him. Cool, resourceful, and physically agile when opportunity strikes, Gerrard embodies supreme presence of mind under mortal peril.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "10": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch10\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">10</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 1: The Road Not Taken</h2>\n      <p>Robert Frost — Choices, Indecision & Destiny — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">Two roads diverged in a yellow wood,\nAnd sorry I could not travel both\nAnd be one traveler, long I stood\nAnd looked down one as far as I could\nTo where it bent in the undergrowth;\n\nI shall be telling this with a sigh\nSomewhere ages and ages hence:\nTwo roads diverged in a wood, and I—\nI took the one less traveled by,\nAnd that has made all the difference.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— Robert Frost</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p1_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p1_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where does the traveler find himself? What problem does he face?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The traveler finds himself in a forest with yellow autumn leaves where the road diverges into two separate paths. His dilemma is that being a single traveler, he cannot travel both paths simultaneously and must make an irreversible choice between them.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Location: Yellow autumn wood at a road fork</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dilemma: Cannot travel both roads; must choose one</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p1_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p1_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What do the following phrases mean in the poem: (i) 'a yellow wood' (ii) 'it was grassy and wanted wear' (iii) 'leaves no step had trodden black'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>(i) 'A yellow wood':</strong> Represents an autumn forest where leaves of deciduous trees have turned yellow before shedding, symbolizing aging, transition, and decay of the year.</p><p><strong>(ii) 'It was grassy and wanted wear':</strong> The path was covered with fresh green grass and needed to be walked upon; it had not been worn down by many travelers.</p><p><strong>(iii) 'Leaves no step had trodden black':</strong> The fallen leaves that morning were fresh and untouched, not yet crushed, bruised, or blackened by human footsteps.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(i) Autumn forest with discolored leaves / transition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(ii) Grassy path that had not been frequently walked upon</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">(iii) Fresh fallen leaves uncrushed by human feet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p1_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p1_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Is there any difference between the two roads as described by the poet in stanzas two and three?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In stanza two, the poet claims that the second road was <em>'just as fair'</em> and perhaps had <em>'the better claim'</em> because it was grassy and less worn. However, he immediately concedes that as far as passing there was concerned, both had been worn <em>'really about the same'</em>. In stanza three, he confirms that both paths lay equally covered with fresh morning leaves untouched by steps, indicating that their physical difference was practically nonexistent.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stanza 2 claim: Second path seemed less worn initially</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Poet's honest admission: Both were worn 'really about the same'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p1_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p1_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does the traveler mean by 'sigh' in the last stanza? What is the deeper metaphorical meaning of the poem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Meaning of the 'Sigh':</strong><br>The 'sigh' may signify regret, nostalgia, relief, or contemplation. The poet realizes that choosing one path means abandoning another forever (<em>'way leads on to way'</em>). Years later, he looks back wondering how his life might have unfolded had he chosen the other road.</p><p><strong>2. Metaphorical Meaning:</strong><br>The diverging roads symbolize <strong>crucial life choices</strong>—career, moral decisions, or personal paths. Every individual must make independent choices. Taking the unconventional, challenging road (<em>'the one less traveled by'</em>) shapes one's character and individuality, ultimately determining the destiny that <em>'makes all the difference'</em>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sigh interpreted: Ambiguity of regret, nostalgia, and acceptance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Metaphor of roads: Diverging pathways and decisions of human life</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Theme: Irreversibility of choices shaping personal destiny</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 10</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Poetic Devices & Theme</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> Identify the rhyme scheme and primary poetic devices in Robert Frost's 'The Road Not Taken'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>1. Rhyme Scheme:</strong> The poem consists of four stanzas of five lines each (quintets) with a strict rhyme scheme of <strong>ABAAB</strong> (wood/both/stood/could/undergrowth).<br><strong>2. Poetic Devices:</strong><br>• <strong>Metaphor:</strong> The diverging roads are an extended metaphor for life's choices.<br>• <strong>Anaphora:</strong> Repetition of 'And' at the beginning of lines 2, 3, and 4.<br>• <strong>Alliteration:</strong> 'grassy and wanted wear' (/w/ sound), 'first for another' (/f/ sound).<br>• <strong>Symbolism:</strong> The 'yellow wood' symbolizes autumn and maturity.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Value-Based / Life Choices</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Reflective Insight:</strong> Why is it impossible to retrace one's steps in life? How does Frost convey this truth?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Frost conveys the irreversibility of human choices through the line: <em>'Yet knowing how way leads on to way, I doubted if I should ever come back.'</em> In life, one decision inevitably opens a series of subsequent paths, circumstances, and consequences that carry an individual further away from the original crossroads. Even if one physically returns to the same spot, one is no longer the same person, nor are the circumstances identical. Hence, decisions must be made with thoughtful deliberation and courageous ownership.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "11": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch11\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">11</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 2: Wind</h2>\n      <p>Subramania Bharati — Inner Strength, Adversity & Friendship with Nature — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">Wind, come softly.\nDon't break the shutters of the windows.\nDon't scatter the papers.\nDon't throw down the books on the shelf...\n\nThe wind god winnows and crushes them all.\nHe won't do what you tell him.\nSo, come, let's build strong homes,\nLet's joint the doors firmly.\nPractise to firm the body.\nMake the heart steadfast.\nDo this, and the wind will be friends with us.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— Subramania Bharati (Translated by A.K. Ramanujan)</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p2_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p2_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the things the wind does in the first stanza of the poem?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In the first stanza, the destructive wind violently breaks the window shutters, scatters papers across the room, throws down books from shelves, tears their pages, and brings torrential rain, creating widespread disorder and wreckage.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Breaks window shutters and scatters papers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Throws books down from shelves and tears pages</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Brings downpours of rain and creates chaos</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p2_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p2_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does the poet say the wind god winnows? What does winnowing mean?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Winnowing Process:</strong> Winnowing is the agricultural process of separating grain from chaff using wind power.</p><p><strong>2. What the Wind God Winnows:</strong> Metaphorically, the wind god winnows and mercilessly crushes all weak structures—crumbling houses, crumbling doors, crumbling rafters, crumbling wood, crumbling bodies, crumbling lives, and crumbling hearts, sparing only what is sturdy and strong.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: Separating heavy grain from light chaff by wind</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Metaphor: Crushing frail houses, doors, bodies, lives, and hearts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p2_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p2_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What should we do to make friends with the wind? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Physical Preparedness:</strong><br>Since the wind god refuses to heed human pleading, the poet advises that we must construct sturdy, strong houses and fasten our doors firmly to withstand furious gales.</p><p><strong>2. Mental and Physical Resilience:</strong><br>Beyond physical structures, the poet urges us to train our bodies to be firm and fortify our hearts with unwavering resolve and courage.</p><p><strong>3. Transformation of Adversity:</strong><br>The poet observes that the wind blows out weak fires but makes strong fires roar and flourish. If we cultivate inner strength and resilience, the ferocious wind ceases to be an enemy and transforms into a loyal friend that strengthens our growth. The friendship of the wind is noble for those who are strong.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Build strong homes and joint doors firmly</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Firm the physical body and make heart steadfast</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fire analogy: Wind blows out weak fires, feeds strong fires; adversary becomes ally</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 11</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Symbolism & Life Skills</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Metaphorical Depth:</strong> How is 'Wind' a symbolic representation of life's struggles and adversities?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>In the poem, 'wind' symbolizes trials, misfortunes, hardships, and economic or emotional crises. Weak personalities collapse under adversity like crumbling houses in a storm. However, individuals with mental fortitude, steadfast hearts, and discipline view challenges as opportunities to grow stronger. Just as fierce wind fan flames into a roaring fire, life's adversities fortify the resilient, proving that inner strength is the ultimate shield against life's tempests.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Poetic Devices</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> Identify the figures of speech employed in 'Wind'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>1. Personification:</strong> The wind is addressed as a living, willful being ('You tore the pages', 'He won't do what you tell him').<br><strong>2. Anaphora:</strong> Repetition of 'Don't' in lines 2, 3, 4 and 'You' in lines 6, 7.<br><strong>3. Repetition:</strong> The word 'crumbling' is repeated seven times to emphasize destruction.<br><strong>4. Symbolism:</strong> Wind symbolizes relentless adversity; fires symbolize human spirits.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "12": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch12\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">12</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 3: Rain on the Roof</h2>\n      <p>Coates Kinney — Memory, Melancholy & Maternal Love — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">When the humid shadows hover\nOver all the starry spheres\nAnd the melancholy darkness\nGently weeps in rainy tears,\nWhat a bliss to press the pillow\nOf a cottage-chamber bed\nAnd lie listening to the patter\nOf the soft rain overhead!\n\nNow in memory comes my mother,\nAs she used in years agone,\nTo regard the darling dreamers\nEre she left them till the dawn:\nO! I feel her fond look on me\nAs I list to this refrain\nWhich is played upon the shingles\nBy the patter of the rain.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— Coates Kinney</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p3_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p3_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does the poet like to do when it rains?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When it rains, the poet considers it pure bliss to lie comfortably in bed inside a cozy cottage-chamber, press his head into a soft pillow, and peacefully listen to the rhythmic patter of gentle raindrops on the shingled roof overhead.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lie in bed in cottage-chamber pressing pillow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Listen to the patter of raindrops on roof shingles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p3_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p3_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the 'single major memory' that comes to the poet? Who are the 'darling dreamers'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Single Major Memory:</strong> The single major memory that enters the poet's consciousness is the tender, affectionate image of his late mother looking down upon him in his childhood.</p><p><strong>2. The 'Darling Dreamers':</strong> The darling dreamers are the poet and his siblings who slept and experienced sweet dreams as children under the vigilant, loving gaze of their mother before she left them until dawn.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Memory: Deceased mother's affectionate face and care</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Darling dreamers: Poet and siblings sleeping as children</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p3_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p3_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Is the poet now a child? Is his mother still alive?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>No, the poet is not a child now; he is a grown adult reminiscing about his bygone youth. His mother is no longer alive, as indicated by the nostalgic phrase <em>'years agone'</em>; she survives only in his tender, sacred memories.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Poet is a grown adult, not a child</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mother is deceased ('years agone'), living only in memory</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p3_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p3_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain: 'Every tinkle on the shingles has an echo in the heart; and a thousand dreamy fancies into busy being start.' (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Acoustic and Emotional Echo:</strong><br>The poet captures the profound psychological connection between sound and memory. Every sharp metallic 'tinkle' of rain striking the roof tiles (shingles) awakens a corresponding emotional resonance and rhythmic echo in the poet's sensitive heart.</p><p><strong>2. Awakening of Imagination and Nostalgia:</strong><br>The sound unleashes a multitude (<em>'a thousand'</em>) of imaginative daydreams and buried childhood memories into active life (<em>'busy being start'</em>). Memory weaves these forgotten threads like air-woven fabric (<em>'thousand recollections weave their air-threads into woof'</em>), resurrecting his mother's loving gaze as he listens to the raindrops' repetitive melody.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sensory tinkle on shingles creating emotional resonance in heart</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Awakening of daydreams and nostalgic childhood memories</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Weaving of recollection threads into vivid mental tapestry</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 12</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Poetic Devices</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Craft:</strong> Identify the onomatopoeia, imagery, and alliteration in 'Rain on the Roof'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>1. Onomatopoeia:</strong> Use of sound-imitating words: <em>'patter'</em> and <em>'tinkle'</em> evoking raindrops on tiles.<br><strong>2. Alliteration:</strong> 'humid shadows hover' (/h/), 'starry spheres' (/s/), 'press the pillow' (/p/), 'cottage-chamber' (/k/), 'busy being' (/b/).<br><strong>3. Transferred Epithet:</strong> 'melancholy darkness' transfers human sadness to night sky.<br><strong>4. Metaphor:</strong> 'rainy tears' comparing rain to weeping tears of sky.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Therapeutic Nature</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Theme of Healing:</strong> How does nature's music heal human grief in the poem?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The poem depicts nature's rainfall not as an inconvenience, but as emotional catharsis. The rhythmic patter of rain acts as acoustic therapy for the adult poet burdened by modern anxieties. It transports him backward through time, re-immersing his weary soul in the comforting, unconditional warmth of his mother's maternal love, proving that nature's rhythms can soothe sorrow and revitalize the human spirit.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "13": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch13\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">13</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 4: The Lake Isle of Innisfree</h2>\n      <p>William Butler Yeats — Solitude, Nature & Longing for Peace — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">I will arise and go now, and go to Innisfree,\nAnd a small cabin build there, of clay and wattles made;\nNine bean-rows will I have there, a hive for the honey-bee,\nAnd live alone in the bee-loud glade.\n\nAnd I shall have some peace there, for peace comes dropping slow,\nDropping from the veils of the morning to where the cricket sings;\nThere midnight's all a glimmer, and noon a purple glow,\nAnd evening full of the linnet's wings.\n\nI will arise and go now, for always night and day\nI hear the lake water lapping with low sounds by the shore;\nWhile I stand on the roadway, or on the pavements grey,\nI hear it in the deep heart's core.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— William Butler Yeats</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p4_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p4_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What three things does the poet want to do when he goes back to Innisfree?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When the poet returns to Innisfree, he wishes to:</p><ol><li>Build a small, rustic cabin made of natural clay and wattles (woven branches).</li><li>Plant nine bean-rows for sustenance.</li><li>Keep a beehive for honey and live in peaceful solitude in the bee-loud glade.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Build small cabin of clay and wattles</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Plant nine bean-rows for simple food</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Have a beehive and live alone in bee-loud glade</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p4_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p4_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does the poet hear and see at Innisfree at midnight, noon, and evening?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>At Innisfree, the poet sees and hears enchanting natural splendor throughout the day:</p><ul><li><strong>Midnight:</strong> Glimmers with the magical, soft light of stars and moon.</li><li><strong>Noon:</strong> Radiates with a romantic, glowing purple heather haze.</li><li><strong>Evening:</strong> Filled with the fluttering sound of tiny linnet birds flying across the sky.</li></ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Midnight: All a glimmer with stars and moon</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Noon: Purple glow of sunlight through heather</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Evening: Sky filled with flutter of linnet's wings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p4_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p4_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where is the poet standing when he hears the lake water lapping? What is 'the deep heart's core'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The poet is standing in the middle of a noisy, crowded city, on asphalt roadways or dull grey pavements (likely London). Despite the urban din, he hears the serene water of Lake Innisfree lapping against the shore in <strong>'the deep heart's core'</strong>—the innermost, spiritual depths of his soul where true peace resides.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Physical location: City roadways and grey pavements</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deep heart's core: Innermost spiritual depths of soul</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p4_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p4_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Contrast the city life of the poet with his vision of life at Innisfree. (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Grim Reality of Urban Life:</strong><br>The poet portrays contemporary city life through drab, somber imagery. The city is characterized by grey, artificial pavements, monotonous roadways, ceaseless traffic noise, mechanical routine, and emotional emptiness, creating intense mental fatigue.</p><p><strong>2. Paradise of Innisfree:</strong><br>In contrast, Innisfree is an idyllic sanctuary of pristine, sensory-rich nature. There, peace <em>'comes dropping slow'</em> like morning mists; crickets sing, bees hum, mornings wear gentle veils, noons glow with purple blossoms, and evenings shimmer with linnet wings. One enjoys complete self-sufficiency through nine bean-rows and a beehive. The contrast reveals a romantic longing to flee artificial urbanization for tranquil communion with mother nature.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Urban depiction: Monotonous, artificial grey pavements, hustle, noise</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Innisfree paradise: Sensory beauty, slow-dropping peace, linnets, solitude</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thematic contrast: Romantic escapism from city stress to nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 13</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Visual & Auditory Imagery</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> How does W.B. Yeats blend visual and auditory imagery in 'The Lake Isle of Innisfree'?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Yeats crafts an immersive musical sanctuary by intertwining sound and sight. Visual imagery includes 'veils of the morning' (dew and mist), 'glimmering midnight', 'purple glow' of midday, and 'linnet's wings' in twilight skies. Auditory imagery complements this with 'bee-loud glade', the cricket's chirp, flutter of wings, and the haunting, hypnotic alliteration of 'lake water lapping with low sounds by the shore'. The liquid /l/ sounds create an auditory trance that echoes the speaker's yearning.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Philosophical Reflection</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Theme of Inner Peace:</strong> Is Innisfree merely a physical place, or a state of mind?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Innisfree is both a real geographical island in County Sligo, Ireland, and a profound psychological sanctuary. The fact that Yeats hears its waters while standing on London's grey pavements shows that Innisfree exists permanently within his consciousness—his 'deep heart's core'. It symbolizes an inner spiritual oasis that modern humans can retreat into when overwhelmed by urban materialism, reminding us that tranquility is cultivated within the spirit.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(14)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "14": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch14\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">14</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 5: A Legend of the Northland</h2>\n      <p>Phoebe Cary — Ballad on Greed, Charity & Saint Peter — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">Away, away in the Northland,\nWhere the hours of the day are few,\nAnd the nights are so long in winter\nThat they cannot sleep them through...\n\nHe came to the door of a cottage,\nIn traveling round the earth,\nWhere a little woman was making cakes,\nAnd baking them on the hearth;\n\nAnd being faint with fasting,\nFor the day was almost done,\nHe asked her, from her store of cakes,\nTo give him a single one.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— Phoebe Cary</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p5_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p5_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What kind of country is 'the Northland'? Why are nights so long there?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">2 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>'The Northland' is a polar, snowbound sub-Arctic country where winter days are extremely brief and nights are bitterly cold and so long that people cannot sleep them through. Reindeer are harnessed to sledges and children wear furry bear clothes.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Polar/sub-Arctic snowbound region with short days</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bitterly cold, endlessly long winter nights; reindeer sledges</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p5_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p5_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What did Saint Peter ask the little woman for? What was her selfish reaction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Saint Peter, famished and exhausted from wandering and preaching all day, asked the little woman for a single cake from her store of hearth-baked cakes. The selfish woman kneaded smaller and smaller dough pieces, but each time she baked one, it appeared too large to give away for free. Finally, she rolled out a dough scrap thin as a wafer, but even that she refused to part with, storing it greedily on the shelf.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Request: A single cake to relieve faintness from fasting</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Selfish behavior: Baking progressively smaller dough portions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Final refusal: Even wafer-thin cake seemed too large to give away</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p5_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p5_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Saint Peter punish the greedy woman? What bird did she become?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Saint Peter grew righteous in anger. He told her she was far too selfish to dwell in human form, blessed with food, shelter, and a warm hearth. He cursed her to become a bird that must build its nest by boring holes into dry, hard wood all day long for scarce food. She flew up the chimney without speaking a word and emerged as a <strong>woodpecker</strong>, retaining only her scarlet cap while her clothes burned black as coal.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Curse rationale: Unworthy of human form, warm fire, and food</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transformation into a woodpecker who bores for scarce food</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Physical detail: Scarlet cap preserved, body charred black as coal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p5_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p5_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a ballad? What moral lesson does 'A Legend of the Northland' impart? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Form of a Ballad:</strong><br>A ballad is a traditional folk poem or song that narrates a story through short, rhythmic four-line stanzas (quatrains), typically featuring a regular rhyme scheme (ABCB). Passed down orally through generations, ballads celebrate folklore, heroic deeds, or moral tales.</p><p><strong>2. Moral Lesson:</strong><br>Phoebe Cary's ballad conveys an enduring ethical warning against <strong>greed, selfishness, and lack of empathy</strong>. When human beings are blessed with abundance (shelter, warm hearth, food), they are morally obligated to share with the hungry and destitute. Greed degrades human dignity; the selfish woman's refusal to surrender even a wafer-thin scrap of bread strips her of humanity, condemning her to the hard, solitary labor of a woodpecker boring wood. The poem urges unconditional kindness and generosity.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ballad definition: Traditional narrative poem in ABCB quatrains</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condemnation of greed and hoarding wealth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Moral duty of charity, empathy, and sharing with the needy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 14</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Moral & Folk Tradition</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Folklore Insight:</strong> Why do old legends like this continue to be narrated to children?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Although the poet admits <em>'I don't believe 'tis true; and yet you may learn a lesson if I tell the tale to you'</em>, legends serve as timeless educational instruments. They instill basic moral codes—such as charity, hospitability, and compassion—in young minds through memorable, vivid symbolism. By turning a greedy baker into a woodpecker tapping endlessly for food, the fable visualizes the spiritual poverty that greed causes, teaching children that hoarding brings misery while sharing brings grace.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Symbolism</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> What does the woman's scarlet cap symbolize?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The scarlet cap is the only surviving human relic of the woman after her transformation. It symbolizes her persistent vanity and arrogance. While all her functional clothing burns away into black soot in the chimney—representing the loss of domestic comforts—the proud scarlet cap remains fixed on the woodpecker's head, serving as a permanent badge of her shame and a warning sign to all who encounter her in the forest.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(15)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "15": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch15\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">15</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 6: No Men Are Foreign</h2>\n      <p>James Kirkup — Universal Brotherhood & Anti-War Philosophy — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">Remember, no men are strange, no countries foreign\nBeneath all uniforms, a single body breathes\nLike ours: the land our brothers walk upon\nIs earth like this, in which we all shall lie.\n\nThey, too, aware of sun and air and water,\nAre fed by peaceful harvests, by war's long winter starv'd.\nTheir hands are ours, and in their lines we read\nA labour not different from our own.\n\nIt is the human earth that we defile.\nOur hells of fire and dust outrage the innocence\nOf air that is everywhere our own,\nRemember, no men are foreign, and no countries strange.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— James Kirkup</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p6_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p6_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does the poet mean by 'uniforms' and 'a single body breathes'? What commonalities unite all human beings?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>'Uniforms' refers to the military uniforms worn by soldiers of different hostile nations, as well as distinct cultural, linguistic, and national garbs. Beneath these superficial outward coverings, the same biological human body breathes. All humans share identical vital needs: the same warming sun, air to breathe, water to drink, nourishment from peaceful harvests, and a shared mother earth where all walk and ultimately lie buried.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uniforms: National military attire and distinct ethnic coverings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Single body: Identical biological and physiological humanity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Shared resources: Sun, air, water, peaceful harvests, final burial in earth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p6_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p6_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are human beings across the world starved by 'war's long winter'? How do their hands resemble ours?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>During peaceful times, all nations thrive on abundant agricultural harvests. However, war acts like a long, harsh winter that freezes productivity, leading to starvation, devastation, and famine for everyone regardless of borders. Furthermore, their hands carry the same calluses and lines of hard daily labor, proving that all people work equally hard to earn their livelihood.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">War's winter: Disruption, famine, and deprivation inflicted on all sides</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hands: Same physical structure, identical lines of toil and labor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p6_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p6_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Whenever we are told to hate our brothers, who tells us? What happens when we take up arms against each other?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Corrupt politicians, warmongers, and self-serving leaders manipulate public sentiments and instruct citizens to hate other nationalities. When we take up arms against our fellow human beings, we do not defeat an alien enemy; we betray, dispossess, and condemn ourselves. War pollutes the innocent air, defiles our shared mother earth with weapons and ashes, and murders our common humanity.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Instigators: Corrupt politicians, warmongers, and power-hungry leaders</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Self-destruction: We betray, dispossess, and condemn ourselves</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ecological defilement: Outrages the innocence of air and defiles earth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p6_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p6_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Discuss the central theme of 'No Men Are Foreign'. How does James Kirkup establish universal brotherhood? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Demolition of Artificial Boundaries:</strong><br>James Kirkup delivers a profound anti-war manifesto asserting the indivisible unity of humankind. Geopolitical borders, flags, and military uniforms are artificial human constructs. In essence, all people belong to a single global fraternity.</p><p><strong>2. Physiological and Psychological Equivalence:</strong><br>The poet demonstrates that all human beings share identical biological organs, eyes that sleep and wake, hearts that respond to love, and hands that toil. We all enjoy the same sun, air, and water, and endure the same misery when war strikes.</p><p><strong>3. Anti-War Philosophy:</strong><br>War is an act of collective suicide. By taking up arms, we poison the common atmosphere and desecrate the holy earth that sustains us. Kirkup ends with an inverted reiteration of his opening line: <em>'Remember, no men are foreign, and no countries strange.'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Core philosophy: Universal brotherhood and rejection of borders</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Universal human traits: Senses, love, labor, shared earth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Anti-war condemnation: War defiles the planet and destroys the self</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 15</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Global Citizenship</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Global Peace:</strong> How does Kirkup's poem resonate with modern international conflicts and peace initiatives?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>In an era marred by war, geopolitical polarization, and xenophobia, Kirkup's poem stands as an urgent humanitarian appeal. It challenges state-sponsored propaganda that dehumanizes enemies. By reminding us that the 'enemy' soldier has eyes that wake and sleep and children who need bread, the poem compels us to recognize our own reflection in the other. True defense lies not in armaments, but in international empathy, trade, and shared stewardship of the planet.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Poetic Devices</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Craft:</strong> Discuss the refrain and metaphors in 'No Men Are Foreign'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>1. Refrain:</strong> The opening line <em>'Remember, no men are strange, no countries foreign'</em> is repeated at the conclusion with subtle inversion (<em>'no men are foreign, and no countries strange'</em>), reinforcing the poem's moral thesis.<br><strong>2. Metaphor:</strong> 'War's long winter' compares war to a barren, freezing winter that starves humanity; 'hells of fire and dust' compares battlefield violence to infernal damnation.<br><strong>3. Imagery:</strong> Sensory details of eyes waking, hands laboring, and breathing bodies emphasize visceral human equality.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(14)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(16)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "16": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch16\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">16</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 7: On Killing a Tree</h2>\n      <p>Gieve Patel — Nature's Resilience vs Brutal Destruction — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">It takes much time to kill a tree,\nNot a simple jab of the knife\nWill do it. It has grown\nSlowly consuming the earth,\nRising out of it, feeding\nUpon its crust, absorbing\nYears of sunlight, air, water,\nAnd out of its leprous hide\nSprouting leaves...\n\nSo hack and chop\nBut this alone won't do it...\nThe bleeding bark will heal\nAnd from close to the ground\nWill rise curled green twigs...\n\nNo,\nThe root is to be pulled out—\nOut of the anchoring earth;\nIt is to be roped, tied,\nAnd pulled out—snapped out...\nAnd then it is done.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— Gieve Patel</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p7_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p7_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Can a 'simple jab of the knife' kill a tree? Why not? What does 'consuming the earth' mean?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>No, a simple jab of a knife cannot kill a tree. A tree has grown slowly over decades by absorbing nutrients from the soil, years of sunlight, air, and water. It possesses immense vitality and deep subterranean roots. Superficial wounds cannot extinguish its deeply anchored life force.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Direct answer: No, simple jab cannot kill a tree</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason: Decades of growth, sunlight, air, water, and deep roots</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">'Consuming the earth': Absorbing vital minerals and nutrients from soil</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p7_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p7_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the meaning of 'bleeding bark'? What makes it bleed? How does it heal?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>'Bleeding bark' refers to the wound inflicted when branches are chopped or hacked by an axe, causing the tree to exude sap that resembles blood oozing from a human wound. The tree has miraculous recuperative powers; its bleeding bark gradually heals, and from near the ground, miniature curled green boughs sprout, eventually expanding back to former size.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition: Sap oozing from hacked tree trunk like human blood</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cause: Man hacking and chopping with axes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Healing: Miraculous self-regeneration from curled green twigs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p7_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p7_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What finally kills the tree? What are the successive steps of destruction?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The tree is finally killed only when its life-source—the root—is completely uprooted from the anchoring earth. The destructive process involves:</p><ol><li>Roping, tying, and pulling the root entirely out of the earth cave.</li><li>Exposing the white, wet, sensitive root to harsh sunlight and air.</li><li>Browning, hardening, twisting, withering, and complete drying out until it is utterly dead.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uprooting the root from the anchoring earth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Exposing sensitive white root to scorching sun and air</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Browning, hardening, withering, and drying out completely</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p7_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p7_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Analyze Gieve Patel's tone in 'On Killing a Tree'. Why does he describe the killing like a surgical procedure? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Sarcastic and Clinical Tone:</strong><br>Gieve Patel, who was himself a practicing medical doctor and poet, employs a dry, clinical, matter-of-fact tone laced with bitter irony. Rather than indulging in sentimental tears, he writes an explicit, cold 'instruction manual' on how to thoroughly murder a living tree, deliberately exposing human cruelty.</p><p><strong>2. Tree as a Sentient Human Entity:</strong><br>He employs somatic, biological vocabulary: the bark is a <em>'leprous hide'</em>, it <em>'bleeds'</em> sap, the root is its <em>'sensitive white heart'</em>, and the earth is an <em>'anchoring womb'</em>. By presenting the tree's execution as a premeditated murder that requires meticulous pulling, snapping, scorching, and choking, Patel forces the reader to confront the sheer violence and horror of deforestation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clinical, ironic, and satirical tone of an instruction manual</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Biological metaphors: Leprous hide, bleeding bark, sensitive white root</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Environmental critique: Exposing barbaric human cruelty against nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 16</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Environmental Ethics</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Deep Ecology:</strong> What does the tree's resilience teach us about nature's enduring power and human destructive capacity?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The poem illustrates that nature possesses astonishing powers of resilience and renewal; a hacked tree sprouts green twigs and regrows. However, man's destructive ingenuity is equally relentless. Humans do not stop at superficial cuts; they dig deep to rip out the roots, destroying the very foundation of life. The poem warns that while nature can heal from casual injury, industrial-scale ecocide destroys roots permanently, reminding society that killing nature is an act of self-extinction.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Poetic Devices</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Analysis:</strong> Explain the significance of the metaphors 'anchoring earth' and 'earth cave'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>1. 'Anchoring Earth':</strong> Compares the earth to a firm seabed where a ship's anchor holds it safe against raging storms, showing how the soil securely cradles the tree's roots.<br><strong>2. 'Earth Cave':</strong> Refers to the deep subterranean hollow where the root system has rested sheltered and hidden for decades. Pulling the root out of this womb-like cave exposes its vulnerable, naked life-source to fatal sunlight.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(15)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(17)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "17": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch17\" data-book=\"Beehive Poetry\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">17</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">BEEHIVE POETRY</div>\n      <h2>Poem 8: A Slumber Did My Spirit Seal</h2>\n      <p>William Wordsworth — Grief, Immortality & Fusion with Nature — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"poem-display-box\">\n    <div class=\"poem-body\">A slumber did my spirit seal;\nI had no human fears:\nShe seemed a thing that could not feel\nThe touch of earthly years.\n\nNo motion has she now, no force;\nShe neither hears nor sees;\nRolled round in earth's diurnal course,\nWith rocks, and stones, and trees.</div>\n    <div class=\"poet-name\">— William Wordsworth</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Poem — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p8_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p8_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does 'a slumber did my spirit seal' mean? What had made the poet oblivious to human fears?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>'A slumber did my spirit seal' means that a state of deep, dream-like sleep or illusion had locked the poet's mind. The poet had loved Lucy so deeply that he fell into a complacency, believing she was immune to aging, disease, and the touch of earthly years, making him entirely oblivious to the mortal reality of death.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Slumber meaning: Dream-like delusion or complacency locking the mind</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Human fears: Blind to mortality, believing beloved immune to aging and death</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p8_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p8_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the poet describe the beloved's present state in the second stanza?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In the second stanza, the beloved (Lucy) is dead and devoid of all physical motion, vitality, hearing, or sight. Yet, she has not vanished into nothingness; she has merged seamlessly with nature, rolled around in the earth's daily diurnal rotation alongside ancient rocks, stones, and trees.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deceased state: Completely motionless, powerless, deaf, and blind</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cosmic union: Rolled in earth's diurnal rotation with rocks, stones, trees</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p8_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p8_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What does 'earth's diurnal course' mean? How does the poet console his grief?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>'Diurnal course' refers to the earth's daily, regular 24-hour rotation on its axis. The poet consoles his profound grief through pantheistic acceptance. Although Lucy has lost individual human consciousness, she has attained cosmic immortality by fusing with the eternal rhythm of mother earth. She is now an immortal part of the planetary fabric—revolving perpetually with nature's timeless elements.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Meaning: Earth's daily 24-hour planetary rotation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Consolation: Pantheistic fusion; attaining cosmic immortality with nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Analytical & Value-Based Questions (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"p8_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('p8_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Analyze the profound contrast between the two stanzas of Wordsworth's eight-line masterpiece. (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. First Stanza (Past Illusion of Immortality):</strong><br>In the opening quatrain, Wordsworth portrays a psychological state of romantic delusion. He was in a spiritual 'slumber' where human mortality seemed irrelevant. His beloved appeared so pure, radiant, and ethereal that he assumed she was beyond the reach of time, decay, and earthly years.</p><p><strong>2. Second Stanza (Stark Reality and Cosmic Transformation):</strong><br>The second stanza presents a devastating shock of reality: she is dead, devoid of motion, hearing, and breath. Yet Wordsworth does not sink into despair. Instead, he elevates her death into pantheistic transcendence. She becomes integrated with <em>'earth's diurnal course'</em>, revolving rhythmically with rocks, stones, and trees.</p><p><strong>3. Conclusion:</strong><br>In just eight minimalist lines, Wordsworth captures the shock of mortality and transmutes painful human grief into sublime cosmic serenity.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stanza 1: Romantic complacency, love making beloved seem immune to death</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stanza 2: Harsh finality of death contrasted with sublime cosmic oneness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Philosophical elevation: Pantheism turning personal grief into timeless peace</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 17</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Pantheism & Romanticism</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Literary Insight:</strong> How does Wordsworth's philosophy of Pantheism shine through this Lucy poem?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Wordsworth's philosophy of Pantheism views God and nature as synonymous. When Lucy dies, the poet does not imagine her in a conventional religious heaven; instead, her body returns to the earth's womb. By asserting that she rolls with rocks, stones, and trees, Wordsworth makes her an eternal participant in the cosmos. Death is not the end of existence, but a return to the infinite, cyclical energy of the natural world.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Poetic Structure</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Form and Rhyme:</strong> What makes this eight-line poem extraordinarily powerful?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The poem's power lies in its extreme poetic restraint and brevity. Written in two simple elegiac stanzas with an ABAB CDCD rhyme scheme and alternating iambic tetrameter and trimeter (ballad meter), Wordsworth uses monosyllabic, everyday words ('motion', 'force', 'rocks', 'stones'). The total absence of melodrama or adjectives gives the poem a solemn, monumental, and haunting dignity.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(16)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(18)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "18": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch18\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">18</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 1: The Lost Child</h2>\n      <p>Mulk Raj Anand — Innocence, Fair Temptations & Filial Bond — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m1_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m1_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the things the child sees on his way to the fair? Why does he lag behind?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>On his way to the spring fair, the child is captivated by a kaleidoscope of sights: toy shops lined along the road, bright dragonflies fluttering their purple wings over a mustard field, little insects and worms crawling along the path, and showers of fragrant young flower petals falling from groves.</p><p>He lags behind his parents repeatedly because he stops in fascination to gaze at each novelty, lost in wonder until his parents call out: <em>'Come, child, come!'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sights seen: Toys, dragonflies, mustard field, insects, falling flower petals</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason for lagging: Fascination and childlike absorption in nature and toys</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m1_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m1_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In the fair, what things does the child want? Why does he not wait for an answer?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In the bustling fair, the child is enticed by several pleasures:</p><ul><li>Mouth-watering sweets (especially his favorite <em>gulab-jamun</em> and <em>burfi</em>).</li><li>A garland of fragrant <em>gulmohur</em> flowers.</li><li>A rainbow-colored cluster of flying balloons.</li><li>The mesmerizing tune played by a snake charmer.</li></ul><p>He does not wait for an answer because he knows from past experience that his parents will dismiss his requests with stern looks or excuses—calling him greedy, calling the garland cheap, or calling the snake music coarse.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Enumerating desires: Burfi, gulmohur garland, balloons, snake charmer</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason for not waiting: Pre-empting parents' routine refusal and excuses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m1_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m1_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">When does the child realize that he has lost his parents? How have his anxiety and insecurity been described?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Moment of Realization:</strong><br>The child watches people enjoying the spinning roundabout. Gathering courage, he makes a bold request: <em>'I want to go on the roundabout, please, Father, Mother.'</em> When no reply comes, he turns around. His parents are nowhere to be seen. He looks all around—front, back, sides—they have vanished.</p><p><strong>2. Visceral Description of Panic:</strong><br>A deep, full cry rises from his dry throat, and with a sudden jerk of his body, he runs frantically in blind terror. Tears roll down from his eyes, hot and fierce. His face is flushed with panic; his turban comes untied and his clothes become soaked and muddy as he runs hysterically through the jostling crowd, shrieking: <em>'Mother, Father!'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Roundabout incident: Bold request met with silence; realization of separation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Physical/emotional panic: Tears, screams, untied yellow turban, frantic running</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m1_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m1_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the lost child lose interest in the things he had wanted earlier? What does the kind stranger offer him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Stranger's Generous Offers:</strong><br>A compassionate stranger rescues the child from being trampled beneath the crowd at the temple shrine. To soothe his hysterical weeping, the man carries him to all the attractions the child had previously coveted—the roundabout swing, the snake charmer, a bright balloon, a fragrant gulmohur garland, and the sweet shop offering sweets.</p><p><strong>2. Refusal and Underlying Truth:</strong><br>The child turns his eyes and face away from every single offering, sobbing inconsolably: <em>'I want my mother, I want my father!'</em> All the glittering material temptations of the fair lose their magic in an instant. The child realizes that worldly pleasures are utterly worthless without the safety, love, and presence of his parents. Parental security is the supreme foundation of a child's universe.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stranger's attempts: Offering roundabout, snake music, balloons, flowers, sweets</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Child's complete rejection: Sobbing only for mother and father</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Theme: Primacy of parental love over material consumerism</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 18</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Child Psychology</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Psychological Insight:</strong> How does Mulk Raj Anand explore child psychology through the shifting desires of the protagonist?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Anand masterfully captures the dual nature of childhood desire. In the security of his parents' presence, the child is intensely curious and attracted to worldly pleasures—sweets, bright colors, toys, and music. However, this desire is entirely dependent on emotional safety. The moment parental security is severed, material desires instantly evaporate, replaced by primal panic. The child's refusal of the very burfi and balloons he cried for minutes earlier demonstrates that for a child, emotional attachment infinitely outweighs physical possessions.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Value-Based Reflection</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Compassion in Society:</strong> What does the role of the unknown stranger signify in 'The Lost Child'?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The unnamed stranger represents the universal spirit of human kindness and empathy. Amidst an indifferent, jostling crowd where the child could have easily been trampled to death, the stranger risks himself, lifts the sobbing boy into his arms, and patiently offers comfort and gifts without asking for any reward. His presence highlights that while the world contains danger and loss, it is also sustained by spontaneous human compassion.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(17)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(19)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "19": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch19\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">19</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 2: The Adventures of Toto</h2>\n      <p>Ruskin Bond — Mischief, Chaos & The Untameable Monkey — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m2_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m2_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does Toto come to grandfather's private zoo? Why was his presence kept a secret from grandmother?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Purchase:</strong> Grandfather bought Toto, a pretty little red monkey, from a tonga-driver for the sum of five rupees. The driver used to keep the monkey tied to a feeding-trough, where Toto looked so out of place that Grandfather decided to add him to his private zoo.</p><p><strong>2. Secret from Grandmother:</strong> Grandmother always fussed and created an uproar whenever Grandfather brought home any new bird or animal. Therefore, Toto's presence was kept a secret until Grandmother was in an exceptionally good mood.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bought from tonga-driver for 5 rupees</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kept secret because grandmother fussed over every new pet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m2_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m2_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe Toto's physical appearance. Why does the author call him a 'pretty monkey'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Toto was an exceptionally attractive baby monkey. He had bright, sparkling eyes deep-set beneath mischievous eyebrows, and pearly white teeth which he bared frequently in a smile that frightened elderly Anglo-Indian ladies. His hands were dried and wrinkled as if pickled in the sun, but his fingers were swift and wicked. His long tail served as a third hand to hang from branches and scoop up treats.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sparkling mischievous eyes and pearly white teeth</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sun-pickled wrinkled hands with nimble, wicked fingers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Long tail serving as a useful third hand</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m2_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m2_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Toto travel to Saharanpur? What happened at the railway turnstile?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Journey in Canvas Bag:</strong><br>Grandfather had to collect his pension in Saharanpur, so he carried Toto in a large black canvas kit-bag with straw at the bottom. Toto could not get his paws through the strong zipper, and when he jumped or rolled inside, the bag rolled about on the Dehradun railway platform, attracting a curious crowd.</p><p><strong>2. Incident at Turnstile:</strong><br>While Grandfather was producing his ticket at the Saharanpur railway turnstile, Toto suddenly poked his head out of the bag and grinned widely at the ticket collector. The startled collector recovered his composure and insisted on charging three rupees extra, classifying Toto as a <strong>dog</strong>!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Traveled in black canvas kit-bag with straw; bag rolled on platform</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Poked head out at turnstile and grinned at ticket collector</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ticket collector classified monkey as 'dog' and charged 3 rupees fare</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m2_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m2_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Toto take his warm bath in winter? How did he once nearly boil himself alive?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Bath Ritual:</strong> Toto cunningly copied the author's bathing routine. He would test the temperature of warm water in a bowl with his hand, step in foot by foot up to his neck, and rub himself all over with soap. When the water cooled, he would leap out and sprint to the kitchen fire to dry himself.</p><p><strong>2. Boiling Kettle Incident:</strong> One day, a large kitchen kettle had been left on the fire to boil for tea. Finding the water comfortably warm, Toto climbed in with his head sticking out. When the water began to boil, Toto hopped up and down, unable to escape because it was cold outside. Fortunately, Grandmother arrived just in time, shrieked, and hauled him half-boiled out of the bubbling kettle!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bath routine: Testing temp with hand, stepping in, using soap, drying at kitchen fire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Kettle incident: Climbed into tea kettle on fire; saved by grandmother when boiling</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m2_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m2_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the author say, 'Toto was not the sort of pet we could keep for long'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Toto was incorrigibly destructive and untameable. He tore wall-paper to shreds, shredded the author's school blazer into ribbons, constantly harassed Nana the family donkey, smashed dinner plates, broke teacups, and threw dishes of pulao down from a jackfruit tree. Grandfather's family was not well-to-do and could not afford the frequent loss of clothes, curtains, dishes, and wallpaper. In despair, Grandfather found the tonga-driver and sold Toto back to him for only three rupees, accepting a loss of two rupees.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Destructive rampage: Shredded clothes, wallpaper, harassed animals, smashed crockery</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Economic reality: Middle-class family unable to bear continual material losses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Resolution: Sold back to tonga-driver for 3 rupees at a loss</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 19</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Humor & Animal Behavior</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Theme of Pet Ownership:</strong> How does Ruskin Bond highlight the responsibilities and boundaries of keeping exotic wild animals as domestic pets?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Ruskin Bond's affectionate humor contains an underlying lesson regarding animal welfare. While Grandfather loves animals passionately, he fails to realize that wild creatures like monkeys are biologically unsuited for domestic confinement. Toto's destructive antics are natural expressions of his wild instincts and boundless energy being trapped inside a human household. By concluding with Toto's return to the tonga-driver, the story gently reminds readers that wild animals belong in their natural habitat, not in living rooms.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Analysis:</strong> Write a character sketch of Grandfather in 'The Adventures of Toto'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Grandfather is an eccentric, kind-hearted, and passionate animal lover with a private zoo comprising a tortoise, rabbits, a tame squirrel, goats, and a donkey. He possesses infinite patience and amusement toward animal antics. Even when Toto destroys wallpaper or shreds a blazer, Grandfather admires the monkey's cleverness rather than losing his temper. However, he is also pragmatic; when Toto's mischief threatens family finances, he realistically recognizes that wild nature cannot be domesticated, making the wise decision to part with the pet.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(18)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(20)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "20": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch20\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">20</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 3: Iswaran the Storyteller</h2>\n      <p>R.K. Laxman — Suspense, Oral Storytelling & Ghostly Delusions — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m3_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m3_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In what way is Iswaran an asset to Mahendra?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Iswaran was an indispensable, loyal asset to Mahendra. He accompanied Mahendra uncomplainingly to all remote construction sites, cooked delicious meals from scratch with ingredients conjured miraculously from nowhere, washed clothes, tidied the makeshift sheds, and entertained Mahendra at night with an endless repertoire of dramatic stories, folklore, and suspenseful tales.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Loyal companion traveling to all remote job sites</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Excellent cook, housekeeper, and laundryman</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Entertainer providing endless dramatic storytelling at night</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m3_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m3_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does Iswaran narrate the story of the uprooted tree? What does it reveal about his storytelling art?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Instead of simply saying a tree had fallen across the highway, Iswaran dramatized the scene with arched eyebrows, spread hands, and dramatic pauses. He described the road as deserted and himself alone, suddenly spotting an <em>'enormous bushy beast lying sprawled across the road'</em>. As he stepped closer, he revealed it was only a fallen tree with dry branches spread out. This reveals his flair for suspense, sensationalism, and vivid theatrical delivery.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Theatrical narration: Arched eyebrows, dramatic gestures, suspense</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Described tree as an enormous bushy beast before revealing reality</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m3_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m3_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does Iswaran recount his encounter with the rogue elephant? How did he supposedly paralyze it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Rogue Elephant Rampage:</strong><br>Iswaran narrated that a tusker escaped from a timber yard, went mad, trampled bushes, smashed fruit stalls in town, broke down school brick walls, and entered the school playground where children hid in classrooms.</p><p><strong>2. Iswaran's Heroic Feat:</strong><br>Claiming he was a brave junior student, Iswaran grabbed a cane from a teacher, marched boldly toward the trumpet-blowing beast, and struck its third toenail with lightning speed. The elephant looked stunned, shivered from head to foot, collapsed, and was knocked unconscious—allegedly paralyzed by the martial art of <strong>karate or ju-jitsu</strong>!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Elephant rampage through town and school playground</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Grabbed teacher's cane and struck the elephant's third toenail</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Claimed karate/ju-jitsu stimulated nervous system to paralyze beast</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m3_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m3_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What story did Iswaran tell about the factory site on a full-moon night? What did Mahendra see that terrified him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Iswaran's Ghost Tale:</strong><br>One full-moon night after dinner, Iswaran claimed that their timber construction site had once been a burial ground. He claimed he frequently saw skulls lying on path, and described seeing an ugly, female ghost on full-moon nights with matted hair, a shriveled dried-up face, holding a fetus in its arms like a skeleton. Mahendra shivered and angrily rebuked Iswaran, calling it a figment of his fevered imagination.</p><p><strong>2. Mahendra's Terrifying Experience:</strong><br>However, on the very next full-moon night, Mahendra was awakened by a low, mournful moaning sound outside his bedroom window. Peering out into the moonlight, he beheld a dark, cloudy shape clutching a bundle! Paralyzed with terror, he broke into a cold sweat and collapsed onto his pillow, panting heavily, unable to shake off the horrifying vision.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iswaran's ghost story: Burial ground, skulls, female ghost holding fetus</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mahendra's rebuke of superstition</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Terrifying sighting: Low moaning, dark cloudy figure clutching bundle in moonlight</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 20</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Power of Suggestion</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Psychological Impact:</strong> Did Mahendra genuinely encounter a supernatural ghost, or was he a victim of psychological autosuggestion?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Mahendra was a rational, educated supervisor with a scientific mindset, yet he fell victim to psychological autosuggestion. Iswaran's hypnotic, vivid descriptions had planted a seed of terror in Mahendra's subconscious. On the full-moon night, when he heard a stray cat yowling in the shadows, his terrified mind projected Iswaran's story onto reality, hallucinating a spectral woman. When Iswaran casually remarked the next morning that he had heard Mahendra's moaning, Mahendra's psychological composure shattered completely, prompting him to resign and flee the site.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Literary Craft</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Humor & Suspense:</strong> How does R.K. Laxman weave humor and gothic suspense together in this story?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>R.K. Laxman blends comedy with gothic eerie suspense. Iswaran's larger-than-life culinary descriptions and absurd claims of felling a rogue elephant with a cane create delightful humor. However, the atmosphere shifts sharply into spine-chilling gothic horror during the ghost story, replete with full moons, rattling skulls, and mournful wailing. By leaving the ending ambiguous—was it a real specter or psychological fear?—Laxman keeps readers both amused and haunted.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(19)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(21)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "21": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch21\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">21</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 4: In the Kingdom of Fools</h2>\n      <p>Kannada Folk Tale (A.K. Ramanujan) — Absurdity, Folly & Wisdom — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m4_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m4_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the two strange rules followed in the Kingdom of Fools?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The King and Minister of the Kingdom of Fools instituted two absurd rules:</p><ol><li><strong>Day and Night Reversal:</strong> People were ordered to sleep during the day and work only after sunset (plowing fields and running shops at night). Anyone disobeying was sentenced to death.</li><li><strong>Equal Pricing:</strong> Everything in the market cost exactly the same price—one single <em>duddu</em> (measure of currency)—whether one bought a bunch of bananas or a sack of pure ghee.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Night made into day; work at night, sleep during daytime</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Everything priced identically at one single 'duddu'</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m4_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m4_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the guru decide to leave the kingdom, while the disciple decides to stay?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The wise guru realized that a kingdom ruled by fools was unpredictable and hazardous, warning: <em>'This is a city of fools; you can't tell what they'll do to you next.'</em> However, the greedy disciple refused to leave because food was incredibly cheap; he could buy mountains of sweets, rice, wheat, and ghee for pennies, wanting to feast and grow fat like a sacred street bull.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Guru's wisdom: City of fools is dangerous and unpredictable</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disciple's folly: Stayed to gorge on cheap food and grow fat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m4_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m4_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Trace the comic chain of blame in the trial after the burglar died.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When an ancient wall collapsed and crushed a burglar, the king initiated a laughable trial where each person shifted responsibility in an absurd chain of blame:</p><ol><li><strong>The Merchant:</strong> Blamed the <em>bricklayer</em> for building a weak wall.</li><li><strong>The Bricklayer:</strong> Blamed the <em>dancing girl</em> whose jingling anklets distracted his attention while laying bricks.</li><li><strong>The Dancing Girl:</strong> Blamed the <em>goldsmith</em> who repeatedly delayed making her jewelry, forcing her to walk up and down the street.</li><li><strong>The Goldsmith:</strong> Blamed a <em>rich merchant</em> whose urgent wedding order forced him to delay the dancing girl's ornaments.</li><li><strong>The Full Circle:</strong> The rich merchant was none other than the original merchant's deceased father, meaning the current merchant inherited his father's guilt and was sentenced to death!</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Merchant -> Bricklayer -> Dancing Girl</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Dancing Girl -> Goldsmith -> Rich Merchant (father)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">King's absurd verdict: Son inherits father's guilt -> sentenced to execution</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m4_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m4_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why was the fat disciple arrested for the execution? How did the Guru save his life? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Why the Disciple was Arrested:</strong><br>When the execution stake was built, the merchant turned out to be too thin to fit the stake properly. Instead of abandoning the execution, the foolish king decreed that a man fat enough to fit the stake must be found immediately. Royal guards rounded up the disciple, who had fattened himself on bananas, rice, and ghee.</p><p><strong>2. The Guru's Ingenious Trick:</strong><br>The guru used his magical foresight, arrived swiftly, whispered a secret plan to his disciple, and staged an enthusiastic quarrel with him over who would be executed first. The guru explained to the king that this stake was the divine stake of the God of Justice; whoever died on it first would be reborn as the King in his next life, and whoever died second would become Minister!</p><p><strong>3. The Foolish King's Choice:</strong><br>Terrified of losing their kingdom in the next life, the King and Minister secretly entered the prison at night, freed the guru and disciple, disguised themselves in their place, and willingly stepped onto the execution stake, getting executed by their own executioners!</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Disciple arrested simply because he was fat enough to fit the stake</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Guru's stratagem: Divine stake prophecy (reborn as King and Minister)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">King and Minister executed in disguise, saving the disciple and freeing kingdom</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 21</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Folklore & Wisdom</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Moral Philosophy:</strong> What timeless moral lesson does 'In the Kingdom of Fools' teach about greed and foolish rulers?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The folktale demonstrates that living in an irrational society governed by foolish leaders is inherently perilous. The disciple's gluttony blinded him to danger; temporary material benefits (cheap food) led him to the executioner's noose. The story warns that when leadership abandons reason, justice becomes a farce. It also celebrates the supremacy of wisdom; the guru's sharp wit and understanding of human psychology outmaneuvered tyranny without violence, proving that wisdom is the only antidote to folly.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Satirical Humour</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Satire on Justice:</strong> How does the trial scene satirize bureaucratic judicial systems?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The trial scene is a biting satire on legal systems that prioritize ritualistic procedural completion over common sense. A burglar dying while committing a crime is absurdly treated as a state murder, and responsibility is tossed from citizen to citizen through ridiculous causality chains. The king's insistence on executing someone—anyone—simply because a stake had been built satirizes bureaucratic obstinacy where procedures must be fulfilled even if innocent lives are sacrificed.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(20)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(22)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "22": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch22\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">22</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 5: The Happy Prince</h2>\n      <p>Oscar Wilde — Compassion, Sacrifice & True Spiritual Beauty — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m5_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m5_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do the courtiers call the prince 'the Happy Prince'? Is he truly happy? What does he see all around him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>When alive, the prince lived in the Palace of Sans-Souci where sorrow was forbidden to enter; he danced and played in gardens, so courtiers called him 'the Happy Prince'. In his statue form, high above the city, he is deeply sorrowful because his eyes can now see all the misery, destitution, and ugly suffering of his people.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Courtiers called him happy because he lived shielded from all sorrow in palace</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Not happy as a statue; weeps upon seeing the poverty and agony of his citizens</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m5_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m5_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the Happy Prince send a ruby for the seamstress? What does the swallow do in her room?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Why Ruby was Sent:</strong> The prince saw a poor seamstress embroidering passion-flowers on a queen's maid's satin gown. Her little boy lay sick with fever in the corner, crying for oranges, but she had nothing to give him but river water. Her hands were pricked and red.</p><p><strong>2. Swallow's Action:</strong> The swallow flew into the room, laid the great ruby on the table beside the woman's thimble, and gently fanned the sick boy's forehead with his wings, cooling his fever into sweet slumber.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Plight of seamstress and feverish boy crying for oranges</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Swallow deposited ruby on table and fanned boy's feverish forehead with wings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m5_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m5_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">For whom does the Prince send the sapphires from his eyes? What happens when both are gone?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The First Sapphire:</strong> Sent to a starving young playwright in a cold garret who is too frozen to finish his play for the theatre director.</p><p><strong>2. The Second Sapphire:</strong> Sent to a poor little matchgirl whose matches had fallen into the gutter and spoiled; her father would beat her if she brought home no money.</p><p><strong>3. The Consequence:</strong> Stripped of both sapphires, the Happy Prince becomes completely blind. Moved to tears by such boundless selflessness, the little swallow vows never to leave the prince and stays beside him forever, despite the deadly winter approaching.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">First sapphire: Freezing playwright in garret to buy food and firewood</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Second sapphire: Matchgirl whose matches were ruined in gutter</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prince becomes blind; loyal swallow refuses to fly to Egypt, staying forever</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m5_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m5_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does the swallow die? What happens to the Prince's leaden heart?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>As winter intensifies, frost and snow coat the city. The swallow grows colder and colder, yet refuses to desert the blind Prince, pecking crumbs outside the baker's door. Knowing death is near, he kisses the Prince on the lips and falls dead at his feet. At that exact moment, a curious cracking sound echoes inside the statue—the Prince's leaden heart breaks cleanly in two from profound grief and love. Even the town foundry furnace cannot melt that broken lead heart.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Swallow freezes to death after kissing Prince goodbye on lips</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Prince's leaden heart snaps in two from immense grief and love</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Broken lead heart refuses to melt in municipal furnace</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">II. Long Answer Questions — Spiritual Nobility (100–120 Words / 6 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m5_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m5_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the 'two most precious things in the city' chosen by the Angel? Why did God praise this choice? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The City Council's Superficiality:</strong><br>When the Mayor and Town Councillors see the statue stripped of gold leaf and jewels, they decree it is <em>'little better than a beggar'</em> and tear it down, squabbling over who should have the next statue erected in their honor. The broken lead heart is discarded onto a dust heap alongside the dead swallow.</p><p><strong>2. God's Divine Verdict:</strong><br>God commands His Angel to bring Him the two most precious things in the entire city. The Angel descends and selects the <strong>leaden heart of the Happy Prince</strong> and the <strong>dead body of the little swallow</strong>.</p><p><strong>3. Eternal Reward in Paradise:</strong><br>God commends the Angel's supreme spiritual wisdom, proclaiming: <em>'You have rightly chosen: for in my garden of Paradise this little bird shall sing for evermore, and in my city of gold the Happy Prince shall praise me.'</em></p><p><strong>Conclusion:</strong><br>Oscar Wilde illustrates that superficial material beauty (gold, rubies, sapphires) decays into dust, whereas selfless love, sacrifice, and empathy are the only eternal treasures recognized in heaven.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrast: Superficial Mayor/Councillors trashing statue vs God's judgment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two precious things: Leaden heart of the Prince and body of the dead swallow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Theme: Selfless sacrifice and compassion are divine and immortal</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 22</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Moral & Spiritual Beauty</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Christian Allegory:</strong> How does 'The Happy Prince' reflect the Christian ethic of self-sacrifice and charity?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Oscar Wilde frames the story as an allegory of Christ-like self-renunciation. The Prince, who was once secluded in earthly luxury, voluntarily gives away his beauty, his sight, and his gold skin to relieve human misery. The swallow acts as a devoted disciple, sacrificing his life and biological migratory instinct out of love for the Prince. Their physical death is not defeat, but spiritual resurrection, proving that giving oneself entirely for the redemption of others transforms mortality into divine grace.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Satire on Vanity</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Social Satire:</strong> How does Wilde satirize the Victorian ruling elite through the Mayor and Councillors?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Wilde exposes the hollow vanity and hypocrisy of the ruling class. The Mayor and Councillors value the Prince only when he glitters with gold, using him as a status symbol to flatter civic vanity. When the Prince sacrifices his ornaments to feed starving children, the elite declare him 'shabby' and melt him down. Their squabble over who should have the next statue highlights political self-aggrandizement, contrasting their spiritual ugliness with the silent nobility of the Prince and the bird.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(21)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(23)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "23": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch23\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">23</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 6: Weathering the Storm in Ersama</h2>\n      <p>Harsh Mander — Youth Leadership, Disaster Relief & Human Resilience — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m6_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m6_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What havoc did the super cyclone of October 1999 wreak in Orissa? How did Prashant survive the initial two days?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Havoc Wrought:</strong> On 27 October 1999, a super cyclone with monstrous winds of 350 km/h struck coastal Orissa. Ancient trees were uprooted, houses washed away, and a raging surge of saltwater inundated Ersama. Dead bloated human bodies and animal carcasses floated everywhere in dark floodwaters.</p><p><strong>2. Initial Survival:</strong> Prashant and his friend's family survived by taking refuge on the open cement roof of their house for two freezing days, subsisting solely on tender green coconuts that had fallen from two coconut trees uprooted onto their roof.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cyclone destruction: 350 km/h winds, flooding, floating carcasses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Survival: Two freezing days on open roof eating tender coconuts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m6_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m6_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Prashant make the perilous journey back to his native village Kalikuda?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Equipped with a sturdy long stick to determine the depth of floodwaters, Prashant embarked on a dangerous eighteen-kilometer trek through swirling, neck-deep waters. He frequently had to push away floating bloated corpses of humans and animals. Occasionally losing the road, he had to swim until he met two friends of his uncle with whom he waded together to his devastated village, Kalikuda.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Used long sturdy stick to test depth of 18 km floodwaters</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pushed away bloated corpses; waded and swam to reach Kalikuda</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m6_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m6_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Prashant organize the youth and community to solve the food and sanitation crisis at the shelter? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Mobilizing Youth to Secure Food:</strong><br>Seeing 2500 survivors starving on green coconuts at the Red Cross shelter, 19-year-old Prashant formed a youth volunteer team. They successfully pressured the local grain merchant to release his stored rice, allowing the camp to cook and eat their first hot meal in four days.</p><p><strong>2. Sanitation and Medical Care:</strong><br>He organized volunteers to clear the shelter of filth, vomit, urine, and floating carcasses, and to tend to the festering wounds and broken limbs of injured survivors.</p><p><strong>3. Attracting Military Relief Helicopters:</strong><br>When military helicopters dropped small rations and did not return, Prashant had children lie on the open sand with empty bowls on their stomachs to signal their desperate hunger. The pilots received the signal and began dropping regular food packets and medicines.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Food mobilization: Pressured merchant to surrender rice for 2500 survivors</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sanitation drive: Cleaned filth, vomit, attended wounds and injuries</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Helicopter signal: Children lying with empty bowls on stomachs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m6_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m6_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Prashant help the devastated widows and orphaned children overcome their grief?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. For Orphaned Children:</strong> Prashant brought all orphaned children together and constructed a polythene sheet shelter for them. He mobilized women to look after them, and later resisted government institutions, successfully resettling orphans within their own community alongside childless widows, creating foster families filled with love and belonging.</p><p><strong>2. For Grief-Stricken Widows:</strong> Seeing women sinking into depression, he persuaded them to join the 'food-for-work' program started by an NGO, and organized cricket matches and sports for children, lifting the community out of the dark chasm of grief.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Orphan care: Polythene shelter, created community foster families with widows</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Widows rehabilitation: Involved them in 'food-for-work' NGO program and sports</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 23</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Disaster Management & Youth Leadership</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Leadership in Crisis:</strong> What leadership qualities make 19-year-old Prashant an inspiring role model for the youth of India?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Prashant exemplifies visionary grassroots leadership. In the face of personal trauma, he rose above his own grief to assume responsibility for 2500 helpless survivors. He showed organizational genius: forming task forces, delegating duties, utilizing psychological insight (engaging widows to care for orphans), and devising peaceful yet firm solutions for food. Prashant proves that real leadership does not depend on age, official power, or money, but on initiative, empathy, and the courage to act when others are paralyzed.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Social Rehabilitation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Community-Based Rehabilitation:</strong> Why did Prashant oppose putting orphans and widows in separate government institutions?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Prashant possessed deep sociological insight. He understood that institutionalizing children in impersonal orphanages causes emotional stunting without love, while isolating widows creates lifelong stigma and loneliness. By uniting childless widows with orphaned children into community foster families, both found emotional healing and mutual purpose, proving that community-based rehabilitation preserves emotional warmth and social dignity.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(22)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(24)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "24": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch24\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">24</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 7: The Last Leaf</h2>\n      <p>O. Henry — Sacrifice, Art & The Will to Live — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m7_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m7_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What was Johnsy's illness? What was the doctor's diagnosis regarding her chances of recovery?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>In cold November, Johnsy caught pneumonia. She lay helplessly on her bed, gazing out the window. The doctor diagnosed that medicines were failing because Johnsy had lost her will to live. She had superstitiously convinced herself that she would die, and the doctor remarked that unless she regained hope, medicines had only a one-in-ten chance of curing her.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Illness: Severe pneumonia during cold November</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Psychological issue: Lost the will to live; convinced she would die</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Doctor's diagnosis: Medicines useless without mental hope</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m7_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m7_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What superstition had Johnsy developed about the ivy vine outside her window?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Johnsy watched an old ivy vine shedding its leaves in the cold autumn wind. She counted them backwards—twelve, eleven, ten... She developed a morbid obsession that when the last leaf fell from the vine, she too would pass away, declaring: <em>'When the last one falls, I must go, too. I've known that for the last three days.'</em></p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Obsession: Counting falling leaves of ivy vine backwards</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Superstition: Firm belief that her life would end when the last leaf fell</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m7_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m7_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who was Behrman? What was his lifelong artistic dream?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Old Behrman was an elderly, sixty-year-old German painter who lived on the ground floor beneath Sue and Johnsy's studio. He had a magnificent long beard curling like Michelangelo's Moses. He was an artistic failure who had drunk gin and talked about painting a masterpiece for forty years, but had never yet begun it.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identity: 60-year-old German painter living on ground floor</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Lifelong dream: To paint a glorious artistic masterpiece</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m7_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m7_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Behrman save Johnsy's life? What happened to Behrman? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Secret Midnight Mission:</strong><br>When Sue told Behrman about Johnsy's morbid fantasy, the old painter was deeply moved. That night, a savage storm raged with driving rain and freezing sleet; the last natural leaf fell. Braving the howling tempest, Behrman climbed a ladder with a lantern and green and yellow paints, painting a realistic, vibrant ivy leaf on the brick wall.</p><p><strong>2. Johnsy's Transformation:</strong><br>The next morning, Johnsy ordered the blind drawn and was stunned to see the lone leaf clinging tenaciously to the vine. When it survived another day of storm, Johnsy realized her despair was wicked and rekindled her will to live.</p><p><strong>3. Behrman's Sacrifice:</strong><br>Meanwhile, Behrman was found in his bed wet, frozen, and helpless with acute pneumonia, his shoes and clothes dripping ice. He died in the hospital two days later, having sacrificed his own life to create his masterpiece—the painted leaf that saved Johnsy.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Midnight painting in freezing storm on ladder with lantern</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Johnsy's realization: Surviving leaf inspires her will to live</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Behrman catches pneumonia and dies; his painted leaf is his masterpiece</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m7_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m7_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does Sue call the painted ivy leaf Behrman's 'masterpiece'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Sue calls the painted leaf Behrman's masterpiece for two profound reasons:</p><ol><li><strong>Artistic Perfection:</strong> The leaf was painted with such exquisite botanical accuracy—dark green near the stem with yellow serrated edges—that both artists (Sue and Johnsy) mistook it for a living leaf.</li><li><strong>Spiritual Triumph:</strong> A masterpiece is defined by its power to transform human souls. Behrman's leaf saved a human life and rekindled Johnsy's dying spirit, created at the ultimate cost of the artist's own life.</li></ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Visual realism: Indistinguishable from a genuine living leaf</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Spiritual impact: Rekindled Johnsy's will to live at the cost of Behrman's life</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 24</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Power of the Human Mind</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Psychological Healing:</strong> How does O. Henry highlight the profound connection between mental state and physical recovery?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>O. Henry demonstrates that the mind holds sovereign sway over bodily health. Johnsy's pneumonia was treatable, but her negative autosuggestion neutralized the efficacy of medicines. When her mental framework shifted from despair to hope upon seeing the unyielding leaf, her immune system rebounded rapidly. The doctor himself confirmed that her chances rose to fifty-fifty the moment she declared a desire to live and paint the Bay of Naples, proving that optimism and hope are the true foundations of physical survival.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Literary Irony</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Classic O. Henry Twist:</strong> Analyze the signature twist ending in 'The Last Leaf'.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>O. Henry is world-renowned for his surprise endings with emotional resonance. Throughout the narrative, readers believe the miracle is a natural phenomenon—a hardy leaf stubbornly clinging to a storm-tossed wall. The revelation in the final paragraph that the leaf was an illusion painted by old Behrman completely recontextualizes the story. The cantankerous, boozy failure is revealed as a noble martyr who traded his life for Johnsy's, transforming a simple story of illness into a sublime testament of love.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(23)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(25)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "25": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch25\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">25</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 8: A House Is Not a Home</h2>\n      <p>Zan Gaudioso — Adolescence, Loss & Rebuilding Community — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m8_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m8_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the author feel awkward, isolated, and strange in his new high school?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The author felt awkward and isolated because all his close junior high friends had gone to a different high school. His new school was twice the size of his old one, making him feel lonely and insignificant. He missed his old teachers and frequently returned to visit them, yearning for the comfortable familiarity of his past life.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Separation from close childhood friends who went elsewhere</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Massive, intimidating new school environment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deep emotional longing for old teachers and past comfort</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m8_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m8_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the fire that destroyed the author's house. What heroic act did his mother perform?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">4 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. The Fire Outbreak:</strong> While Zan was doing homework on a cold Sunday afternoon with his red tabby cat purring on his papers, smoke began pouring through the ceiling seams. Within seconds, thick smoke filled the room, forcing Zan and his mother to flee into the front yard as flames engulfed the roof.</p><p><strong>2. Mother's Heroic Act:</strong> Zan's mother bravely sprinted back into the burning inferno to retrieve a small metal box containing vital family documents. Then, crazed with grief, she ran back inside a second time to rescue letters and photographs of his late father, the only tangible memories of his existence, before being pulled out by firemen.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Smoke engulfing house, forcing escape into front yard</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mother running inside to rescue metal box of documents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mother running in second time for late husband's photos and letters</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m8_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m8_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the author say: 'I was grieving for my cat, but I had to go to school looking like a geek'?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>The day after the fire, Zan had to attend school without shoes (they had burned), wearing borrowed oversized tennis shoes, mismatched clothes, and carrying no backpack, notebook, or homework. Feeling stripped of dignity and overwhelmed by grief over his missing red cat, he felt completely out of place, wanting to curl up and vanish.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Loss of all belongings: No shoes, backpack, books, or uniform</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Profound sorrow and despair over missing beloved cat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m8_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m8_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did the students and teachers of the new high school surprise the author? What does the title 'A House Is Not a Home' mean? (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">5 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Generous Outpouring of Compassion:</strong><br>When news of the fire spread, the students and teachers gathered at a table in the gym to present Zan with a massive collection of school supplies, notebooks, backpacks, and clothes—jeans, tops, sweats. Students who had never spoken to him introduced themselves, invited him to their homes, and welcomed him warmly into their community. For the first time, Zan felt hope and made real friends.</p><p><strong>2. Meaning of the Title:</strong><br>The title draws a profound philosophical distinction:</p><ul><li>A <strong>'house'</strong> is merely a physical structure of brick, wood, and mortar that can easily burn to ashes.</li><li>A <strong>'home'</strong> is an emotional sanctuary forged by love, family, companionship, community, and belonging.</li></ul><p>When his cat was safely returned by a kind woman and friends surrounded him, Zan realized that his physical house was being rebuilt, but his true emotional 'home' was restored within his heart.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">School community's generous gift: Clothes, school supplies, genuine friendship</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Distinction: 'House' (physical building) vs 'Home' (love, belonging, community)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Return of cat symbolizing total restoration of life and happiness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 25</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Emotional Resilience</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Adolescent Vulnerability:</strong> How does Zan Gaudioso capture the fragile mental state of teenagers navigating loss?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Gaudioso captures the painful insecurity of adolescence with raw honesty. Moving to a new school already induces anxiety; losing one's home, clothes, and childhood cat compounds this into existential dread. Zan feels naked, humiliated, and disconnected from life. The narrative shows that teenage suffering often stems from feeling unloved or invisible. When the school community embraces him with tangible kindness, his emotional armor dissolves, demonstrating that empathy and belonging are the ultimate remedies for youth depression.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Value-Based Reflection</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Kindness of Strangers:</strong> What role did the kind woman play who found the cat?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>The woman who found the terrified cat went to extraordinary lengths to trace its owner. Even though the phone number on the collar was disconnected due to the fire, she persistently researched and located Zan. Her selfless integrity mirrors the generosity of the high school students, reinforcing the theme that human goodness surrounds us even in our darkest hours, ready to restore what was lost.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(24)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(26)\">Next Unit →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n", "26": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch26\" data-book=\"Moments Supplementary\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">26</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">MOMENTS SUPPLEMENTARY</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 6/9: The Beggar</h2>\n      <p>Anton Chekhov — Redemption, Labor & Olga's Selfless Compassion — CBSE Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">I. Thinking about the Text — Short Questions (30–40 Words / 2–3 Marks)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m9_1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m9_1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What lies did the beggar Lushkoff tell Advocate Sergei to extract money from him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Lushkoff told pathetic, conflicting lies. On one day, he begged for charity claiming he was an innocent rural schoolteacher who had lost his post through village intrigues. On another day, he told Sergei he was an expelled student who had an offer of work in the province of Kaluga but had no money for the journey.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Claimed to be an impoverished rural schoolteacher</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Claimed to be an expelled university student needing fare for Kaluga</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m9_2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m9_2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Advocate Sergei catch the beggar lying? What work did Sergei offer him?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Sergei recognized Lushkoff because two days earlier he had seen the same man in Sadovaya Street begging under the guise of an expelled student, whereas now he claimed to be a schoolteacher. Furiously threatening to call the police for swindling, Sergei offered him honest manual labor: <strong>chopping wood</strong> in his yard for half a ruble.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sergei recalled seeing him in Sadovaya Street with contradictory story</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Threatened with police, then offered wood-chopping work in his yard</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m9_3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m9_3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Was Lushkoff willing to chop wood? Why did he agree to do it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>No, Lushkoff was not at all willing to chop wood. His physical strength was thoroughly undermined by vodka, and he was weak, sluggish, and had no appetite for hard manual labor. He agreed to go along solely out of shame, trapped pride, and because his lies had been publicly exposed by Sergei.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Unwilling due to alcohol-induced physical weakness and laziness</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Agreed out of wounded pride, embarrassment, and trapped situation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m9_4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m9_4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Where did Sergei meet Lushkoff two years later? What had Lushkoff become?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">3 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p>Two years later, while standing at the ticket window of a theatre paying for his seat, Sergei noticed Lushkoff standing beside him. Lushkoff had transformed into a clean, respectable, and well-dressed notary earning thirty-five rubles a month.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Meeting: Theatre ticket counter two years later</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transformation: Clean, respectable notary earning 35 rubles a month</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1½ Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"m9_5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('m9_5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Q5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Who was truly responsible for reforming Lushkoff—Advocate Sergei or Olga the cook? Discuss. (Max Word Count)</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">6 Marks</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Standard Answer (Max Word Count)</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Sergei's Superficial Role:</strong><br>Advocate Sergei believed that his stern rebuke and strict insistence on honest physical labor had transformed the beggar. He took self-righteous pride in having set Lushkoff on the right path, reminding him that he had provided the opportunity and tough love.</p><p><strong>2. The Shocking Truth (Olga's Selfless Sacrifice):</strong><br>Lushkoff confessed to Sergei that it was not Sergei, but his cook, <strong>Olga</strong>, who saved his soul. Whenever Lushkoff arrived to chop wood, Olga scolded him fiercely, calling him a drunkard and a wretch. But then she would weep bitterly, looking into his face, crying: <em>'You miserable creature! There is no happiness for you in this world, and in the next you will burn in hell!'</em></p><p><strong>3. True Transformation Through Love:</strong><br>Moved by deep, selfless pity, Olga chopped every single log of wood herself for Lushkoff, allowing the drunkard to collect the wages without lifting a finger. Her tearful sacrifices, unconditional compassion, and selfless labor pierced Lushkoff's hardened heart, inspiring him to stop drinking, reform his character, and become an honorable man.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sergei's role: Provided tough opportunity, but remained aloof and prideful</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Olga's secret: Chopped all the wood herself while weeping for his soul</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Moral core: Stern lectures fail; unconditional empathy and sacrifice redeem human beings</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency-Based & Value-Based Questions — Unit 26</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #0284c7;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#0284c7;\">Compassion vs Discipline</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Theme of Human Redemption:</strong> How does Chekhov show that love and empathy are far more powerful than judgment and discipline?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Chekhov explores the psychology of moral redemption. Sergei represents institutional justice and bourgeois morality—he shames the beggar and demands work, feeling self-congratulatory pride. However, shaming someone only drives them further into defensive deceit. Olga, on the other hand, embodies Christ-like love. Beneath her harsh, scolding tongue lies profound sorrow for Lushkoff's wasted life. By secretly taking his punishment upon herself and doing his labor, she awakens his buried conscience, proving that genuine transformation occurs through grace and sacrifice, not through harsh judgment.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #059669;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Character Sketch</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\"><strong>Character Analysis:</strong> Contrast the characters of Advocate Sergei and Olga the cook.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p>Advocate Sergei is respectable, wealthy, righteous, and somewhat pompous. He is eager to take credit for Lushkoff's salvation, congratulating himself on his moral discipline. In contrast, Olga is a coarse, humble domestic cook whose exterior is gruff and foul-tempered, but whose soul is pure gold. She seeks no praise, no payment, and never boasts of her charity. Her tears and secret labor are acts of pure, anonymous grace, making her the true spiritual hero of the story.</p></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(25)\">← Previous Unit</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <div></div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n"};
